// The boss journal: Volkar, in each hero's own words (docs/plans/journal-voices.md)
(window.JOURNAL_DATA = window.JOURNAL_DATA || {}).volkar = {
  // the first time this hero meets him (after the first pull)
  meet: {
    bastion: "No door to hold, no wall at my back: a mountaintop with weather from every side and a gold or blue charge crawling over each of us. I set my feet and found the footing was wind. The sergeant never trained me for that. Report: we need a new drill.",
    gloomsworn: "Volkar never left his summit. The wind came at us from every side while a gold or blue charge crawled across each of us. I have been run through by a blade. Weather is slower, and it does not stop at the wound.",
    thresher: "After the ogre's kitchen and the spiders' cellar, open sky! He hung a charge on each of us, gold or blue, like a ribbon on a hat, and never stirred from his summit. I tipped my hat to him, a guest whose turn is coming, and hummed the whole climb.",
    bloodreaver: "A mountain with lightning for blood! After the ogre's cleavers and the queen's fangs, every bolt left a mark, and between the bolts the wind kept biting. I counted bruises until I ran out of fingers. Old thunder-belly and I are going to be friends.",
    runeguard: "A fresh page for Volkar: cloud before the clap, sparks along the floor before the blow, charges that pair and arc. A long text, legible, though written in a hand like forked lightning. I left the margins wide for my successor.",
    gorgemaw: "A kitchen on a mountaintop, in the rain, where every dish is lightning and the salt is blown in on the wind. Grubnak at least owned a stove. I rather admired the austerity. I left hungry, which is the point of austerity.",
    oathbroker: "My counterparty is a mountain that will not move, and he binds each of us to a charge, gold or blue, before negotiation begins. The rate of interest was not disclosed. I have read the terms twice; the storm reads faster.",
    ironpalm: "The mountain does not walk. It speaks from where it stands, and under its voice the storm murmurs without pause. I listened a long time before I answered anything.",
    ranger: "Big. Doesn't move. Lets the weather do its running. After the spiders' dark, a summit with no cover worth the name, and wind that changes its mind every few breaths. First time all year I wanted a roof.",
    shadowblade: "A mark who never turns round, because the whole sky does his looking. After the queen's webs, where every dark corner had eyes, here is a place with none, and I stood lit like a candle in a window. Dreadful manners.",
    pyromancer: "VOLKAR! A whole storm in one enormous body, and his lightning is better than mine! Wind in my sleeves, rain on my best fire. I have never been so insulted, or taken so many notes.",
    thermist: "Cold rain, hot lightning, never one long enough to read properly! I ran icy, then scalding, then icy again, notebook soaked through. Fascinating. Appalling. I lost the middle on that summit and did not find it till dawn.",
    driftblade: "He never takes a step, and the wind runs for him, through the rocks and round us and back again, and at last something on this earth moves more than I do. I was thrilled for about a breath, and then I was blown flat.",
    marionettist: "Tonight's leading man never leaves centre stage; he is more or less the stage, and the weather works the flies. After the ogre's kitchen and the queen's lair, a new venue, and the cast spent the first act finding its marks in a gale. (The Doll lost a ribbon.)",
    cleric: "Volkar hardly moves. He needn't; the storm wears every one of us down a little at a time and never lets up. I prayed on that mountain until my voice went, and the young ones had to be helped down. I carried the tea. It was the least I could do.",
    grovekeeper: "Old Thunder isn't a beast, dear, he's weather with a face. Wind, rain and lightning, building all season. My lot came down soaked and humbled, and I found a snail in my sleeve. I was rather pleased about the snail.",
    chronomancer: "A single long piece, the same every time, and only ever louder. I knew the first bars, which was unsettling, since I am fairly sure it was our first hearing. Fairly. The thunder will correct me.",
    alembist: "Specimen: Volkar, one mountain, damp. After the ogre's kitchen I thought I knew about hot; he is lightning, rain in the reagents, and wind lifting the labels off my bottles. Half a shelf spoiled. The red bottle survived. Phew.",
    threadweaver: "After the spider queen's webs I thought I knew about threads. The old windbag shoved us apart and yanked us together all fight long, gold to blue, and every thread I tied the wind tried to snap. I scolded my stitches, then mended them, in that order.",
    tidecaller: "A mountaintop is only a headland with the sea drawn out of it, and all the weather here was his. He hung a charge on each of us, gold or blue, like a ring on a gull's leg, and I kept the water high and watched which way the sky leaned.",
    lanternkeeper: "The summit was all his weather. He hung a charge on each of us, gold or blue, and I lit lanterns on the wrong ground twice before I saw how the colours pair. Tomorrow I learn his round before I light it. Keep your light.",
    kenseier: "A mountaintop, and weather with a rhythm to it, if one waits. Each of us wore a charge, gold or blue, and his fist came down toward me slow as a bell. I mistimed the first two and heard about it from the sky. Catch the beat.",
    houndmaster: "Bare mountaintop and a sky that changes its mind. Gold or blue on each of us, and the big lad's fist coming down slow. Ash wouldn't stop looking up. Neither would I. Back tomorrow, with a better plan.",
  },
  // the first time this hero beats him
  win: {
    bastion: "He fell. The wind was still shoving as he went, bad footing to the last. I counted heads on the summit, every one present, then let my knees shake where only the mountain could see. Held.",
    gloomsworn: "The sky came down on us again and again, and we held it up between us until he went down. The mountain is quiet. I stood the whole way, and it is a long while since standing felt like something I had chosen.",
    thresher: "He is down, and the sky has gone quiet. Every one of us walked down that mountain, which is more than most storms allow, and I hummed the whole way. I said a few kind words over him, as one does. His name is struck from the list.",
    bloodreaver: "He went down still crackling, and I carried every bolt he gave me down the mountain like medals. Best bruises of my life. Old thunder-belly, you were magnificent, and I will find another like you.",
    runeguard: "The last page of the Titan's text was his own fall, and I had half-read it some while before. The storm runs to its final line and goes quiet. I have inscribed a colophon in my own hand. As foreseen.",
    gorgemaw: "The sky was the last course, and we ate it together, all of us at one table, rain in the soup. Then the kitchen went quiet. A long meal, honest and bracing. I am still hungry, and I take that as a compliment to the cook.",
    oathbroker: "The account is closed: the sky was paid for in common, to the last instalment, and the storm defaulted first. I have signed away a great many defeats. This was the first discharge I enjoyed. Countersigned.",
    ironpalm: "The mountain fell silent. The wind kept speaking a while, as an echo does, and then it too stopped. I stood in clear air with nothing left to answer.",
    ranger: "The old stag went down in the rain. Never moved once, start to finish. Neither did my last arrow, after it left. Clean line. Dry shirt, finally.",
    shadowblade: "The mark crumbled into the clouds, still staring straight ahead. In all that weather it never once saw me, which is the nicest thing about a mountain. I took the long way down. Alone, naturally.",
    pyromancer: "I outshone the storm! Only at the very end, only briefly, but the clouds parted to look. The price: one eyebrow, my hat, and a certain amount of dignity. The dignity went earlier.",
    thermist: "He fell and everything cooled at once. Rain on my face, no swing left in me either way. Reading: dead centre, for the first time in my life. I stood in it until someone told me to move.",
    driftblade: "He fell, the wind dropped, and I wheeled round the summit anyway, through the rain and out the other side, just because nothing was left to push me but me.",
    marionettist: "Curtain! The mountain brought the house down, quite literally, and the troupe held up the sky together for the finale. (The Doll bows; I accept the flowers; the wind kept the programmes.)",
    cleric: "Volkar fell, and every one of us walked down that mountain on our own feet. I have never prayed so long, nor so happily. My old bones ache and I could not be more content. Thank the light.",
    grovekeeper: "The storm broke and the sky cleared, the way weather does when you outlast it. My lot stood close under the last drops, steaming like horses. A long season, and a good harvest.",
    chronomancer: "He fell at the end of his long piece, and for once I needn't hear it again. Or I did, many times, and this was the last. The silence is the part I do not recognise. Pleasantly new.",
    alembist: "Result: Volkar, cured. Treatment: patience, in many doses, and everyone together at the end. Shelf bare, workshop intact, one small fire, entirely deliberate. I intend to sleep for a week.",
    threadweaver: "The windbag fell and the threads held, every one, the whole hem drawn tight under the falling sky. Not a sloppy seam left on that mountain. I sat down and put the needle away, which I do not do lightly.",
    tidecaller: "He went down and the wind went with him, slack water at last on a high place. Every one of us walked off that mountain, and I let the tide run out of me slowly. The sea gives back.",
    lanternkeeper: "The storm went out of him slowly, like a wick running dry. All four of us walked down off that mountain, and I left a lantern burning on the highest stone, where the weather cannot reach it. I'll leave one burning.",
    kenseier: "Volkar is down and the wind has stopped trying to move me. His fist had the slowest beat I have ever answered, and the longest silence after. I stood in it a while.",
    houndmaster: "Sky's quiet. Rust's shaking the rain off, Ash is asleep on my boots, and my coat will never be dry. Good dogs. Had him by supper, and a wet one.",
  },
  moves: {
    'Thunderclap': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Cloud over the summit, light gone. A clap that reaches every man cannot be blocked, so I stopped trying and stood beside the healers like a man at a funeral. It was only loud. Headcount after: all present.",
      gloomsworn: "The light drained out of the sky and the clap went through all of us at once. There was nothing to stand behind. I let the healers fill us beforehand and took the rest as it came. It was only noise, and I know quiet.",
      thresher: "Clouds went over the summit and the light went with them, which is how a proper downpour sends word ahead. The clap landed on every one of us at once, no shelter to be had, so I let the menders fill me first. In time!",
      bloodreaver: "Sky went black and then cracked open over all of us at once. A decent sting! I stood there grinning while the others winced. Come to it whole, then drink the rest back on the next few blows.",
      runeguard: "Provisional reading: when the summit darkens under rolling cloud, the clap that follows reaches everyone and cannot be stepped out of. Each wound must be closed beforehand. Easy to foresee. Harder to bear, but I managed.",
      gorgemaw: "When the clouds roll in, the cook serves everyone the same plate at the same instant. It cannot be sent back, only swallowed, so we came to table full beforehand. Thin broth, but the whole room had to drink it.",
      oathbroker: "Article the first: the clap is owed by every signatory at once, no corner of the summit exempt, and it falls on top of what the storm has already taken. The only available defence is arriving whole. We did.",
      ironpalm: "The clouds gathered. Then the clap, for everyone at once. There was nowhere to step, so I stood ready and let it pass through the bones. Some words are not dodged. Only heard.",
      ranger: "Sky went dark. Clap hit everyone, no lane out of it. Came in mended, kept shooting through the ringing. Rude weather.",
      shadowblade: "The clouds rolled over and he clapped at the whole room, and there was no corner dark enough to be unseen in. So I arrived whole, like anyone sensible, and took it standing.",
      pyromancer: "The sky went dark, the only time all night anything was dimmer than me, and then it BOOMED across everyone at once! No dodging it. I had been mended first and kept casting through it, ears ringing, hat gone.",
      thermist: "Clouds. Dark. Clap, everywhere at once! No corner escapes it, so be whole beforehand: that is the entire experiment. I ran icy with dread, kept casting, shook out the ringing like water.",
      driftblade: "There is no way through the clap, it rolls over the whole summit at once, and I hate a thing I cannot slip round, so I kept circling and let the healers fill me before the clouds closed overhead.",
      marionettist: "House lights down (clouds; a lovely effect), and then the thunder struck the entire house, stalls and wings. No exits. The cast simply arrived whole for its cue. A tolerable entr'acte.",
      cleric: "When the clouds roll over the summit, the clap is meant for everyone, and no mending afterwards is as kind as mending before. So I did. Nobody went down. I like it best when the work is finished early.",
      grovekeeper: "The sky darkens like a summer storm coming up the valley, and then the clap reaches all of us. I'd planted in my lot as the first cloud came over, so it was already mending when it struck. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "Clouds, then the clap, then ringing: always in that order, and it reaches everyone. So I mended them all while the sky was still darkening, which is, or will be, just enough. I seem to be good at clouds.",
      alembist: "Dose everyone during the darkening, not after, because the clap reaches the whole summit and there's nowhere to hide a person. Red bottle, the big one, poured over the lot of us as the clouds closed. Ears still ringing. Cork popped.",
      threadweaver: "You cannot darn a sound. The clap pulled on every thread at once, so I had the whole party mended and tied together before the clouds closed. It tugged each stitch and not one snapped. Good cloth, this lot.",
      tidecaller: "The sky darkened and a great clap reached all of us at once, with no corner of the summit to hide in. I had the water at the crest as the clouds closed, so it broke on a full shore and nobody went down.",
      lanternkeeper: "The clouds closed over the summit and a clap meant for all of us was on its way, with no corner to hide in. I let every lantern burst as the sky went dark, and the party stood in glow upon glow. It struck a room already mended.",
      kenseier: "The light drained from the sky, and a clap went through all of us at once. There is nothing to cross with a blade in thunder. The healers filled us beforehand and I stood in the open and took it. It was only loud.",
      houndmaster: "Sky went dark, then a clap through all of us. Nowhere to hide from weather. Came to it mended, dogs at my knee, and just stood in it. Only loud.",
    } },
    'Titan Fist': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Lightning ran down his arm toward me. I waited for the shoulder to drop and braced in the last instant, knowing the blow would not stop at me, and sent the others to the flank, out of the path behind. A shield is for the man in front. Everyone else needs orders.",
      gloomsworn: "Lightning crept down his arm toward me. I let the fist fall, drew the dark in tight at the last, and kept the others out of the path behind. It does not stop at the first body. I am used to being the first.",
      thresher: "Lightning ran up his arm toward our knight, and the fist was due. It goes down a long line, through the knight and out the other side, so I took my place at his flank, well off that line, and let it arrive to an empty room. Tidy.",
      bloodreaver: "Lightning crawled down his arm and the fist came for me alone. A good one! I took it full and hit back while it still burned, and kept the others out from behind me, where it carries on. Share a bruise, not a hole.",
      runeguard: "Lightning gathers along the line between the Titan and me, and the blow does not end where I stand. A ward a breath before, and nobody else in that line. On the harder climb it leaves me charged; I decline the next rod. Written in the margin.",
      gorgemaw: "A signature dish, sent down a long crackling table to me alone. I opened wide just as it landed, and made sure no one sat behind me to share it. Peppery, I'll grant him. Seconds welcome.",
      oathbroker: "Delivery of the fist is due to whoever stands before him, and I accepted it with my guard raised. Rider: the blow continues past the recipient, so no co-signatory may stand at my back. I do not guarantee another man's injuries.",
      ironpalm: "Lightning gathered along his arm toward me. I raised my guard only in the last instant, and the others stood clear of the line behind me. It asked. I answered.",
      ranger: "Fist comes down a long lane, through our shield and out the other side. Kept to the flank, well off that line. Nobody behind the knight, nobody hurt. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "His fist was meant for our shield, and the lightning ran on past him in a long line, like a rumour. I waited at the side, where neither fist nor eye could find me.",
      pyromancer: "Lightning poured down his arm toward our knight and on past him! Glorious, and not for me, so I kept well to the side of that line and never stopped casting. Not a hair lost, for once. I checked three times.",
      thermist: "Lightning down his arm, toward our shield. Hypothesis: the blow runs on behind him. Confirmed! Not there! Not there! I stood to the side, burning, while it went past hot enough to taste.",
      driftblade: "His fist is for our shield, but the lightning carries on through him and out the far side, so I swung wide to the flank, out of that lane, and cut back in the moment it passed, because standing behind a knight is only waiting.",
      marionettist: "A big moment for the lead, aimed at our knight, and the blow carries on upstage behind him. I kept my Doll off that line, safely to the side. (The knight took his bow. Bruised, but upright.)",
      cleric: "When lightning gathers along his arm, the heavy blow is coming for our knight. I laid the light on him before it fell, and scolded the young archer for standing behind him. It runs on, child. Dawn keep him.",
      grovekeeper: "Lightning creeps down his arm toward our shield like frost along a branch, dear. I'd planted on the shield before it, and kept my lot off the line behind. It mended as it landed.",
      chronomancer: "The fist lands when his arm stops glowing, and not before. I had our shield mended in the gap, and stood off the line behind him, where the blow keeps going. Nobody needed undoing. This time.",
      alembist: "One enormous blow for our shield, carrying on in a line behind him, which I learned by standing there. Blue bottle for the shield beforehand, everyone else out of the line. I have labelled that stretch of floor: contents under pressure.",
      threadweaver: "The fist went for our shield and ran on down the line behind him. I kept every stitch of mine off that line and ran a thread to the shield to share the weight. Cloth tears where it's pulled, my loves. Mind where you stand.",
      tidecaller: "Lightning crept down his arm toward our knight, and a swell like that does not stop at the first rock; it runs on up the beach. I kept the others off the line behind him and sent the water to the knight just before the fist fell.",
      lanternkeeper: "Lightning crept down his arm, and the blow was for our knight. I hung a lantern on him before the fist came down, and the rest of us kept off the line behind him, since the bolt runs on past.",
      kenseier: "Lightning crept down his arm toward me. I let the fist come, met it with the blade as it fell, and the others stayed clear of the path behind. A slow blow, and very honest about its beat. Breathe, then strike.",
      houndmaster: "Lightning crept down his arm. Fist then came down a long lane straight through the shield-man. Kept out of that lane, off to the side, and held the hounds back too.",
    } },
    'Polarity Surge': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Gold or blue on each of us. Orders, then: pair off, opposite colours, shoulder to shoulder, and keep your own kind away, since two of a kind arc and burn. I found my blue and stood by him like a man on parade.",
      gloomsworn: "Gold or blue, each of us. When the surge came I went to the opposite colour, near enough to touch, and kept my own kind away. Two of a kind in one place is how it kills. Opposites cancel. I could say something about that.",
      thresher: "Gold and blue, like two partners at the start of a dance. When the surge came due I stood beside one of the other colour, near enough to touch, and kept my own colour well away. Like beside like, and the whole line scorches.",
      bloodreaver: "Two golds together make a terrible arc; one alone, a nasty bite; gold beside blue, barely a tickle. Shameful! But I found my blue and stood close anyway, grumbling. Bruises are for choosing.",
      runeguard: "Arcs crackle between like charges before the surge, and a calm thread joins unlike ones; I drew it in the margin. Each of us stands beside an opposite charge, with no like charge within reach. Simple, once annotated.",
      gorgemaw: "The surge is a pairing dish: gold with blue, never gold with gold, and never eaten alone at a table for one. I sat beside my blue companion and it came out almost bland. An excellent pairing, if I say so.",
      oathbroker: "Each gold requires a blue to countersign beside him; a second gold within reach voids the instrument and arcs through both. I read my partner's side of the contract as carefully as my own, and we both survived the audit.",
      ironpalm: "Gold must stand with blue. Two golds argue, loudly. One alone shouts into the wind. I found my blue and we stood quietly together. Silence between opposites. That is the whole lesson.",
      ranger: "Paired with a blue, shoulder to shoulder, nobody of my colour in reach. Other pair kept their distance. Didn't need to be told twice.",
      shadowblade: "Gold must keep company with blue, never its own kind and never alone, which is a very public sort of rule. I stood beside my opposite at a polite distance from anyone like me. Charming arrangement.",
      pyromancer: "I was gold that night, naturally! Standing near another gold made the air scream between us, so I stood close to a nice calm blue instead, and the surge barely tickled. Disappointing. Safe, though.",
      thermist: "Sorting pairs: blue beside me, gold kept away, nobody alone. Then the surge, and the heat between us just fizzled! Like charges, violent; unlike, inert. Beautiful. Note it down. Don't let me forget.",
      driftblade: "I had to stand still beside my opposite, which I hate, so I wheeled until the arcs began to crackle and then dropped in right next to my blue, close enough to touch, well away from anyone gold.",
      marionettist: "A duet: each gold paired with a blue, close, and the duets kept apart. I took my place beside my partner on cue. (The Doll watched from the wings and approved of the staging.)",
      cleric: "Gold beside blue, near enough to hold hands, and no other gold close by. The young archer drifted toward her own colour once and arced dreadfully. I mended her and said nothing, which she understood.",
      grovekeeper: "Gold and blue pair like rain and soil. My lot sorted themselves into couples, each gold rooted beside a blue, the couples kept apart. The surge went over like a mild breeze.",
      chronomancer: "I found my opposite before the surge, stood close, and kept my own colour out of reach. The arcs between like charges are the warning; I heeded them. Frequently. Possibly in advance.",
      alembist: "Mixing guide: gold and blue. One of each, close together: inert, lovely. Two of a kind: violent. One alone: still bad. So everyone beside one opposite! (I did not need to test this. I tested it anyway.)",
      threadweaver: "Gold tied to blue, close, a short thread between, and no two golds in reach of each other. Anyone left alone took the whole surge. Pairs, like socks: lose one and the other's no use.",
      tidecaller: "Gold or blue on each of us, and like charges fight each other the way two rips do. I went and found my opposite, close enough to touch, and kept all of my own colour well away from me.",
      lanternkeeper: "Gold or blue on each of us, and like colours fight. I went to my opposite and stood close enough to share one glow, and kept every other of my own colour at arm's length.",
      kenseier: "Gold or blue on each of us. Opposite colours went together, close enough to touch, and kept their own kind away, since two alike arc and burn. I found my partner and stood beside him. Opposites cancel.",
      houndmaster: "Gold or blue on each of us. Unlike colours stood together, like colours stood off, for two of a kind spark and burn. Found my opposite and went shoulder to shoulder. Dogs kept out of it.",
    } },
    'Thunder Crash': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A cloud over each gold. My blue partner and I stood under mine and split the crash, and the other pair kept their distance. Then everybody scattered from the static, fast, no marching. The one order I enjoyed giving.",
      gloomsworn: "Clouds hung over the gold ones. My blue partner and I took the one over mine and split the crash, while the other pair stood far off. When it passed we scattered from the static. Half a crash is survivable. I have survived worse halves.",
      thresher: "A cloud darkened over each gold one of us. Each pair stood under their own and took the rain together, the pairs a good distance apart, two neat little parties. Then the static came in behind, so we broke off, every one to their own ground.",
      bloodreaver: "A cloud over my partner's head. I stood right under it with her and took my half, a good meaty half, then scattered before the static. Half a crash each. Fair, for a mountain.",
      runeguard: "A storm cloud over every gold. Each gold's blue partner stands beneath it to share the blow, the pairs well apart; and then, at once, everyone scatters, for static follows hard behind. I underlined the word scatters.",
      gorgemaw: "A cloud over each gold head: a sharing plate. My partner and I split ours down the middle, the other pair kept to their own table, and then we all left our seats before the static arrived. Generous portion.",
      oathbroker: "Joint liability, drawn at last: each gold with his blue under a single cloud, each pair at a distance from the other, and everyone dispersing at once, since the static is a rider on the crash. Entered in the ledger.",
      ironpalm: "A cloud over the gold. My partner and I stood together and took it as one, and the other pair stood elsewhere. Then we parted, quickly. Shared weight is half weight.",
      ranger: "Clouds over the golds. Each pair stood under theirs, pairs well apart. Then everyone scattered. Static doesn't wait.",
      shadowblade: "A little cloud followed each gold one of us about, how flattering. I tucked in under mine with my partner, away from the other couple, and we slipped apart the instant it broke.",
      pyromancer: "A thundercloud hung over my head like a bad mood! My blue partner stood under it with me, we split the crash, and then we scattered before the static. Hair: frizzled. Spirit: unbroken.",
      thermist: "Cloud over my partner. Step in, under it with her, other pair far off. Crash, shared, half each, lovely arithmetic! Then hot, fast, scatter before the static. I got three paces. Three!",
      driftblade: "A cloud over my partner meant landing beside her and staying there, which I endured, the other pair far across the summit, and the moment it broke I was away through the gap, light as a kite, before the static could find me.",
      marionettist: "Scene: a cloud over each gold player. Blocking: each blue joins their gold beneath it, one pair upstage, one down. Then exeunt in every direction. A crisp little scene.",
      cleric: "The clouds gathered over the gold ones, and each blue partner stood under with them, the two pairs well apart, so the weight was shared. I mended both pairs as they scattered from the static. A kind arithmetic, that.",
      grovekeeper: "Clouds over the golds, like squalls over two fields. Each pair rooted together under their own, the fields well apart. Then everyone scattered like seed, before the static came down.",
      chronomancer: "Under the cloud in pairs, the pairs apart, and then scatter just after the crash, because the static is right on its heels. I keep hearing the whole business as a single bar of music.",
      alembist: "Two heavy bottles, one over each gold: each pair shares its own cloud, pairs kept well apart, and everybody scatters the instant it lands, because the static is steeping behind it. Ask me how I learned the pairs-apart part. Actually don't.",
      threadweaver: "Each gold had a cloud, so I tied each blue to their gold, close, and kept the pairs well apart. Then I cut every thread at once and they scattered before the static. Snip, snip, away you go.",
      tidecaller: "Clouds gathered over the golds, and each blue went to stand under their gold's cloud so the weight was shared, the two pairs far apart. When it broke we all ran from the static like spray off a rock, and I mended in the running.",
      lanternkeeper: "Clouds gathered over the golds, and each blue went in under their gold's cloud to share the weight, the two pairs well apart. When it broke we all ran from the static. Two pairs, two lanterns, one for each.",
      kenseier: "A cloud hung over each gold one. My blue partner and I took the crash together under mine, and the other pair kept off. Then everyone scattered from the static. Shared, it was bearable. Alone, it would not have been.",
      houndmaster: "A cloud hung over each gold one. My partner and I stood under it together, other pair well off, then everyone scattered from the static. Shared, it's a knock. Alone, it's a funeral.",
    } },
    'Static Field': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Static on every head, and each needed ground to themselves. I took my patch, planted my boots and kept the others in sight. Nobody crowded. Whatever else you say of the line, it knows its spacing.",
      gloomsworn: "Static took hold of every one of us, and each needed room apart. I found my own bare place and stayed on it. A man who has been buried does not mind a small space.",
      thresher: "Static crept onto every head together, and each of us wanted a patch with a gap round it. I wandered out to my corner and let it go over me, humming. Dear little spark, do hold still. Nearly done.",
      bloodreaver: "A ring of static over every head. Mine alone, nobody else's in it. I took my own patch and stood there dying of boredom while it fizzled. Even I won't bleed on a friend.",
      runeguard: "A ring of static gathers over every head, and each ring wants clear ground of its own. No ring may touch another; I sketched the spacing on the flyleaf, and the party fanned out accordingly. Elegant, and survivable.",
      gorgemaw: "Every guest got a little plate of static, and nobody should eat off their neighbour's. I kept to my own end of the table. Dull, but tidy, like a meal in a monastery.",
      oathbroker: "Each of the undersigned is liable for his own static, severally and not jointly. We stood apart, each settling his own account. It is rare to be glad of a clause that separates us.",
      ironpalm: "Static gathered over each of us. Each took their own ground, far from the others. Quiet. No one spoke over another. That is rare, in a storm.",
      ranger: "Rings over every head. Fanned out, each to our own ground. Nobody touching. Then back together, when it went off.",
      shadowblade: "Static over every head, each ring wanting a patch of floor to itself. I took a quiet corner of my own, as ever. Solitude suits me. Everyone else had to learn it.",
      pyromancer: "A crackling ring over my head, and one over everyone else's too! I took my own patch of the summit, far from anyone, and glowed in peace. Good riddance, audience. Back soon.",
      thermist: "Static over everyone. Answer: apart. Each alone, no ring on another. Then breathe, warm up, close in again. It took me five attempts to land the spacing right. Five!",
      driftblade: "Static over every head, so I slipped out to my own patch of the summit, nobody's ring touching mine, and the instant it snapped I was back through the middle, grateful to be moving.",
      marionettist: "Everyone gets a solo. Each player took their own spot on the stage, well apart. (Nobody upstaged anybody, for once. A historic night.)",
      cleric: "Static gathered over every head. Each of my friends took a patch of ground apart from the rest, and I went to them one by one afterwards, mending. A small walk, in the circumstances, and a long one for my knees.",
      grovekeeper: "A ring of static over each head. My lot spaced themselves like seedlings, each in their own bed, nobody's roots crossing. Then we gathered back up, dear, and dusted off.",
      chronomancer: "Static over every head; it follows the crash by a moment, which I am sure I had already noticed. Each of us to our own ground, far apart, and together again afterwards. Rehearsed.",
      alembist: "Shelf rule: nothing volatile touches anything else. Static on each of us spills onto anyone near, so everyone apart, one apiece, single doses afterwards. Lesson from the old workshop. Ceiling still missing.",
      threadweaver: "Each of my stitches carried a ring of static, and those must never overlap. I let out all the slack, everyone apart, then drew them back in once it cleared. Tidy. Needle between my teeth the whole while.",
      tidecaller: "A ring of static sat over every head, and rings that touch only make a worse sea. Each of us took a patch of floor alone, spaced like gulls along a rail. When it cleared I walked the shingle of them and topped up whoever had been nicked.",
      lanternkeeper: "A ring of static hung over every head, and rings that touch make it worse. Each of us took a patch of floor alone. When it cleared I went round with my lantern and topped up whoever had been nicked.",
      kenseier: "Static took hold of each of us, and each needed room apart. I found my own bare patch and stayed on it. A blade needs room to move, and so, it turns out, does everything else.",
      houndmaster: "Static took hold of each of us. Each wanted a bare patch of their own, so we spread like beaters and stayed put till it went off. Held the hounds out of everyone's way.",
    } },
    'Rod Strike': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A ring burned down over my head. I put my back to the nearest standing stone and the bolt struck the rock, not me. The next pair took other stones, since a struck stone needs time. I have worn worse armour than granite.",
      gloomsworn: "The ring sat on my head like a crown. I went to the nearest stone, turned my back to it, and the lightning went into the stone. Next pair, different stones; a stone struck once needs a rest. Not unlike me.",
      thresher: "A ring settled on my head, which meant the bolt was close. I stepped back against the nearest standing stone and the lightning went into it, tidy as you like. The next pair used different stones, since a struck one needs resting, as a blade does.",
      bloodreaver: "Marked! I ran to a standing stone and the bolt hit the rock instead of me. A pity. The next pair went to different stones; strike one twice in a hurry and it bursts on everyone near.",
      runeguard: "A burning ring over a head: that person stands against a standing stone, and the bolt goes into the rock. The second pair must choose untouched stones, since a freshly struck one discharges. I wrote untouched twice, and underlined it.",
      gorgemaw: "A bolt meant for me, so I carried it to a standing stone and let the rock be the plate. The second pair used fresh stones; a used one spits back at the table. Once, mouth open, I simply swallowed mine.",
      oathbroker: "Marked, I conveyed the bolt to a standing stone, which assumed the liability. A stone lately struck cannot bear a second claim, so the next pair must use the others. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "The ring burned over my head. I walked to a stone and stood against it. The sky spoke to the rock, not to me. The next two found other stones.",
      ranger: "Marked. Ran to the nearest standing stone, shoulder to it. Bolt took the rock. Second pair went for the other stones, not ours.",
      shadowblade: "A ring over my head: the sky had noticed me, how rude. I leant against a standing stone and let it take the bolt. The next pair chose fresh stones; a struck one sulks and lashes out.",
      pyromancer: "A ring burned down over my head! I dashed to a standing stone, hugged it, and the bolt struck the rock instead of my lovely hair. The next pair went to the other stones, sensibly.",
      thermist: "Marked: a ring burning down. Nearest stone, back against it, others away. Bolt into the rock, hot through my teeth. Next pair: fresh stones. Never the same one twice. Twice and it bites back!",
      driftblade: "A ring burning down over my head meant run, for once, straight to a standing stone, and stop against it so the bolt struck the rock, and the next pair slipped past us to the other stones.",
      marionettist: "Cue: a ring over two heads. Those players each take a standing stone and stand against it; the bolt plays to the rock. The second pair take fresh stones. (The rest of the cast keeps clear.)",
      cleric: "Two of my friends were marked, and each took their bolt against a standing stone. The next pair took the other stones, for a stone struck twice so soon bursts on everyone near. Thank the light for rocks.",
      grovekeeper: "A ring over a head is lightning looking for a tall tree. My marked ones stood against the standing stones and let the stone take it; the next pair chose fresh stones. Never strike the same tree twice.",
      chronomancer: "Two marked, then two more a little later. Each goes to a stone and lets the bolt find it; the second pair uses the stones the first left alone, since a struck stone remembers. I have been struck before, I think.",
      alembist: "Ailment: a bolt marked for me. Remedy: stand against a standing stone and let the rock have it. A used stone stays charged a while, so the next pair uses fresh ones or it spoils for everyone near. Hugged mine. Slightly warm.",
      threadweaver: "The marked ones walked to a stone each and stood right against it. The next pair went to other stones. Everyone else kept their hem well clear of the struck rocks. Never press cloth to a hot iron.",
      tidecaller: "Two of us wore the marks, and each stood hard against a standing stone so the bolt broke on the rock like a wave on a cliff. The next pair went to other stones, since a stone just struck is not ready to be struck again.",
      lanternkeeper: "Two of us were marked, and each stood against a standing stone so the bolt broke on rock. The next pair used different stones, since a stone just struck is not ready again. I kept my lantern between the stones, within reach of all.",
      kenseier: "A ring settled over my head. I could not turn a bolt, so I put the nearest standing stone between us, and it went into the rock. The next pair used different stones; a struck stone must rest.",
      houndmaster: "A ring settled on my head. Ran to the nearest standing stone and put my shoulder to it, and the bolt took the rock. Next pair used other stones. A struck stone needs rest.",
    } },
    'Gale Slam': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He drew breath. Where a rod stood I stepped into the still air behind it, away from him; with none, I closed in on him so the throw stopped short of the rocks. Charge the enemy, my sergeant said. For once it was safe advice.",
      gloomsworn: "He sucked in a breath and the wind threw us outward. If a rod was near I sheltered in the calm behind it, the side away from him; if none, I closed in beside him and flew only a short way. I have been thrown farther by worse.",
      thresher: "He drew breath, and a gale to scatter ash was coming. With a rod near I tucked in on its far side from him; with none I stood right at his knees, so the throw ran out before the rocks. Close is the safe seat, for once, dear.",
      bloodreaver: "Old thunder-belly breathed and threw us at the walls. Slammed into stone? Not even an honest wound. So: behind a standing rod, out of the wind, or close in, where the throw lands on open floor.",
      runeguard: "The Titan draws breath. The still air stands behind a rod, on the side away from him; with no rod, one stands near enough that the throw ends short of wall and stone. I recorded both readings, since it will come again.",
      gorgemaw: "A dish made of wind alone, and it hurls the diner across the room. One eats it in the lee of a standing rod; with no rods, close to the cook, so one lands nowhere hard. All air, no substance.",
      oathbroker: "The gale's terms: everyone is thrown outward, and whoever is flung into stone pays dearly. The exemption clause is the still air behind a planted rod. Failing rods, stand close enough to him that the throw ends on open floor.",
      ironpalm: "He breathed in. I stood in the stillness behind a rod, and the wind went past. When no rod stood, I stepped close to him, and landed lightly. Breath in, breath out.",
      ranger: "He breathes, the wind throws you outward. In a rod's lee, you don't move. No rods: stand in close, so the throw runs out before the rocks.",
      shadowblade: "He took a great breath and flung everyone away. I tucked into the lee behind a rod, like a cat behind a curtain. No rods? Stand close to him and land softly. Ungainly, otherwise.",
      pyromancer: "He inhaled like a bellows and hurled us all outward! I hid in the still air behind a standing rod and kept casting. With no rods up, I stood close so I landed on open floor, not rock. Sleeves: ruined. Dignity: eventually.",
      thermist: "He drew breath. Behind a rod, in its quiet strip, on the side away from him. Or close in when there are none, so the throw falls short of stone. Then the wind, hot and enormous, and I didn't move!",
      driftblade: "He breathed and the wind threw everyone and I wanted to fly with it, truly, but the rocks argued, so I tucked in behind a standing rod, out of his breath, and with no rods I went in close so I'd land short.",
      marionettist: "Stage direction: the leading man inhales; the cast is blown into the scenery. Better blocking: stand behind a planted rod, in its still air. With no rods, stand close to him, so the throw stops before the flats.",
      cleric: "He drew a great breath and the wind threw us outward. My friends sheltered behind a standing rod, away from him. When none stood, we kept close to him, so nobody was dashed against stone. Odd, to seek safety beside the thing that frightens you.",
      grovekeeper: "The gale came on like a spring blow off the peaks. My lot sheltered in the lee behind the rods, like seedlings behind a wall. With no rods, we stood close in, so the wind's throw fell short.",
      chronomancer: "He breathes in, and a moment later the wind throws everyone outward. Behind a standing rod nothing moves you; with no rods, close to him, the throw ends before the rocks. I have been thrown enough times to know.",
      alembist: "Everyone thrown outward, hard into any rock: shelter behind a standing rod, on the side away from him, or if there are none, stand close, so the landing is open floor. Do not shake the bottles during landing! I shook them.",
      threadweaver: "When he breathed I drew everyone in behind the standing rods, where the air was still. With none, I pulled the party in close round him, so the throw ended before the rocks. Tight, tight, tight. Pulled it tight.",
      tidecaller: "He drew a long breath, and a breath that long goes out as a gale that throws us outward. I stood in the still lee behind a rod, away from him. With none standing, I stayed close in beside him, where the throw ran out before the rocks.",
      lanternkeeper: "He drew a long breath that would throw us outward. We sheltered in the lee of a standing rod, with the rod between us and him. When none stood, we kept close in beside him, where the shove ran out before the rocks.",
      kenseier: "He drew breath. Where a rod stood I sheltered in the still air behind it, on the side away from him; where none did, I closed in on him so the throw stopped short of the rocks. The nearest place was the safest.",
      houndmaster: "He drew breath and the wind threw us outward. Behind a rod, out of the wind, you stand still. No rod, go in close, so the throw runs out before the rocks. Dogs go in under my coat.",
    } },
    'Storm Walls': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Walls of wind swung round him slowly, like spokes. I kept my place in the gap and marched with them, step for step, and stood behind a boulder where one lay. It was almost drill.",
      gloomsworn: "Walls of wind swung round him slowly, like cartwheel spokes. I found the gap and shuffled round with it. A boulder nearby was something to put between me and the wall. I was glad of the company.",
      thresher: "Walls of wind went round him slow as a clock hand. I found the gap between two spokes and walked along with it, unhurried, humming. A boulder made a fine bit of shelter, once. Never rush a turning wheel.",
      bloodreaver: "Walls of wind wheeling round him! I stood in the gap and walked with it, hitting him the whole way round. A wall would sting, but walking is free.",
      runeguard: "Lines of wind are drawn outward from the Titan and turn. One stands in the gap between them and walks with the turning, never against it; a boulder's lee holds, but only once. Pedantic, perhaps. Also alive.",
      gorgemaw: "The revolving dessert trolley: walls of wind, turning. I walked with the gap between them, never into them, and sheltered behind a boulder when one had been laid. Overwhipped, yet refreshing.",
      oathbroker: "The walls revolve on fixed terms, slowly and in one direction. We walked within the gap at their pace. A boulder shelters from a single pass, and is then spent; a second claim against it will not be honoured.",
      ironpalm: "Walls of wind turned around him. I walked with them, in the space between. Slow steps. When a boulder lay there, I let it speak for me.",
      ranger: "Walls turn round it like a mill wheel. Pick a gap, walk with it. Boulder's lee works, once.",
      shadowblade: "Walls of wind wheeling round the mark. I walked with them in the gap, like a shadow across a sundial, keeping pace. A boulder hid me, once, which is more than most hiding places give.",
      pyromancer: "Turning walls of wind! I had to walk while casting, which is undignified, keeping to the gap between them as they swept round. Behind a boulder I could stand still, gloriously, once.",
      thermist: "Walls, turning. Find the gap, walk with it, keep pace. Too fast, the wall ahead; too slow, the one behind. Dead centre of the gap, that's the reading. Easy to say. Terrible to walk.",
      driftblade: "Walls of wind turning round him are only a route that moves, so I glided round with them inside the gap, never through a wall, and tucked behind a boulder when there was one.",
      marionettist: "A revolving stage! The cast walked with it, between the walls, and my Doll kept her place in the gap. Boulders made a fine screen, for one turn. (Reviewers praised the choreography.)",
      cleric: "The walls of wind turned slowly round him. My friends walked with them in the gaps, and those behind boulders were sheltered once. I mended as we walked. Slow work, for old legs.",
      grovekeeper: "The walls turn like a slow wind round a hill. My lot walked with them, in the gaps, at the walls' own pace. A boulder gave shelter for one pass, no more.",
      chronomancer: "The walls turn at an even tempo, and walking with them in the gap is rather like keeping time. A boulder shelters for one pass, and then it is gone. I have walked this circle already, I believe.",
      alembist: "Walls of wind turning slowly round him: walk in the gap between, at their pace. A boulder shelters from one pass, then spoils. I tried a second pass. Not recommended. Eyebrow still regrowing.",
      threadweaver: "The walls turned round him and I kept the party in the gap, walking together, nobody lagging into the wall behind. A boulder covered us once. A lagging stitch gets caught. Mind the slack.",
      tidecaller: "Walls of wind swung slowly round him, and I walked in the gap at their own pace, the way a tide follows the moon. A boulder gave shelter once, for a single pass. There was no hurry, though the wall behind kept its own time.",
      lanternkeeper: "Walls of wind swung slowly round him. We walked in the gap at the wall's own pace, and a boulder sheltered us once, for a single pass. I trailed a lantern behind me, so the party kept to the gap by its light.",
      kenseier: "Walls of wind swung round him slowly, like spokes. I kept to the gap and went round with it, behind a boulder where one lay. The slowest beat I know, and easy to miss if you stare at your feet.",
      houndmaster: "Walls of wind swing round him like spokes. Found the gap and walked round with it, boulders at my back where there were any. Slow as a plough team. Don't stare at your feet.",
    } },
    'Chain Lightning': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "A bolt gathered over our healer while his fist came at me. She walked clear of everyone, and the rest of us stood well apart, so it had nobody to leap to. I took the fist alone. Two duties, one pair of hands. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "A bolt gathered over our healer as his fist came for me. She walked away from everyone and the others kept apart, so it had nothing to jump to. I took the fist myself. It was mine to take.",
      thresher: "A bolt gathered over our healer while his fist fell on the knight. She strode off alone, and the rest of us spaced out well apart, a polite distance each, so the lightning had nowhere to jump. A good gap is a kindness.",
      bloodreaver: "Lightning over our healer's head, itching to jump. She walked off alone and I kept my distance from her and everyone. Hard not to share; I love to share. But every leap hurts someone new.",
      runeguard: "A cell gathers over one of us, ringed by how far it can leap. That one walks clear of everyone, and the rest stand apart from each other besides, so the bolt finds no second body. I diagrammed the ring. It is a good ring.",
      gorgemaw: "A dish that hops from plate to plate. Its first diner walked away from the table; the rest of us sat far apart. Once, mouth open, I let it hop into me and end there. Still hungry.",
      oathbroker: "A bolt bound to one of the undersigned passes to whoever stands nearest. Remedy: everyone outside its reach and apart from one another, so that the debt goes no further than its maker.",
      ironpalm: "Lightning gathered over our healer. She walked away. The rest of us kept our distance, each alone. The bolt had no one to talk to.",
      ranger: "Storm over the healer. She walked off; the rest of us kept our own distance. Bolt had nowhere to jump.",
      shadowblade: "A storm cell crept over our healer. She drifted off alone, gracefully, and the rest of us kept our distance from her and from each other. Lightning cannot leap to a shadow.",
      pyromancer: "A storm cloud over our healer, with a ring showing how far it could leap! She walked off on her own, and we all kept away from her and from each other. I stood at the very edge, nobly, hat in hand.",
      thermist: "Bolt over our healer. Everyone out of her ring, and out of each other's. She walked alone. It struck her and found nobody else! Zero spread. Zero! I could weep.",
      driftblade: "A bolt gathered over our healer so I swept wide, out past the ring that showed how far it leaps, nobody else near me either, and it struck her and found no path through the rest of us.",
      marionettist: "A solo for our healer: the bolt hangs over her, and the cast leaves her the stage, everyone far apart. (My Doll waited in the wings.)",
      cleric: "A bolt gathered over me just as the fist fell on our knight. I walked away from everyone and asked them to keep apart. It struck me once and stopped. Better an old man than the young ones.",
      grovekeeper: "A cloud over my own head, building like a thunderhead on a hot day. I walked off from my lot, they kept apart from each other, and it struck me alone. I'd mended myself already, dear.",
      chronomancer: "The bolt gathers over me as the fist lands on our shield; two things at once, which I dislike. I walk off from everyone, the others keep apart, and it strikes only me. I will have mended the shield on the way.",
      alembist: "A bolt over me, wanting to leap! Walk away from everyone, and the others keep apart too. Dose the shield before walking, since his fist lands at the same moment. Without a label, I'd have missed it.",
      threadweaver: "The bolt gathered over me, so I cut every thread and walked off alone, and the others kept well apart. It struck once and found nothing to run along. A cut thread conducts nothing.",
      tidecaller: "The bolt gathered over my own head while his fist fell on our knight, so I walked away from everyone and the rest kept well apart, leaving it nothing to leap to. It struck me once and went out like a spent wave. I had filled my own water beforehand.",
      lanternkeeper: "On the hard nights the bolt gathered over my head just as the fist fell on our knight. I walked away from everyone, lamp and all, and the others kept apart to give it nothing to jump to. It struck me once and went out.",
      kenseier: "A bolt gathered over our healer while his fist came for me. She walked away from everyone and the rest of us kept apart, so it had nothing to leap to. I met the fist alone. Two beats at once, one pair of hands.",
      houndmaster: "A bolt gathered over the healer. She walked off alone and the rest of us kept apart, so it had nowhere to jump. Kept the dogs apart too. Close dogs carry sparks.",
    } },
    'Lightning Rods': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Iron rods in the rock, each wanting one of us when the bolt fell. I took one, and stood out of the next while I still crackled. The rods stayed up after, good cover. I would have posted a guard on them.",
      gloomsworn: "Iron rods were set in the stone, each hungry for one of us when the bolt came. I took one, then sat out the next while I still crackled. They stood on after, as shelter. Nothing wasted.",
      thresher: "Iron rods stood in the stone, each waiting on one of us for the bolt. I took a rod, then let someone else have the next while I still crackled, taking turns like a good dance. Good shelter afterwards, those rods. I would have kept them.",
      bloodreaver: "A rod for each of us to catch. I took one and the bolt went straight through me. Lovely. The next rod I left alone; twice running would floor even me. Probably.",
      runeguard: "Iron rods are driven into the rock, and one of us belongs in each before the bolt falls. Whoever is still charged from a rod stands aside for the next. An empty rod strikes us all; I underlined empty.",
      gorgemaw: "Rods of iron, one per guest, each serving a single bolt. I took mine. A second helping while still buzzing from the first is poison, so I left that course to others. No rod goes uneaten.",
      oathbroker: "Each rod must be answered by exactly one signatory. Whoever caught the last is still charged and must not sign again; an unanswered rod defaults on everyone. Surety, in short, is for the strong, the rested and the willing.",
      ironpalm: "Rods struck the rock. One of us stood in each. The one who answered last time rested. Afterwards the rods remained, quiet shelter from the wind.",
      ranger: "Rods went in. One of us to each. Whoever caught the last one sits out. Rods stay standing; useful cover after.",
      shadowblade: "Iron rods, each wanting a body when the bolt falls. I took one, still and patient, then let someone fresh take the next. Leave one empty and it strikes us all, which would be vulgar.",
      pyromancer: "Iron rods hammered into the summit, each begging for a lightning bolt, and of course I volunteered! Next time I stood aside, still fizzing. One bolt in a while is plenty, even for me.",
      thermist: "Rods. One each, nobody still charged from the last. The bolt hit and I ran hot right through, then let the next rod go to someone cooler. Reading after: still humming. Teeth, too.",
      driftblade: "A rod for each of us meant standing in one, still, until the bolt came through me, then letting somebody fresh take the next, and afterwards the rods stood there as shelter from his breath.",
      marionettist: "Casting call: one player per rod, never whoever played the last one. An empty rod brings the house down on us all. (The rods stayed on as scenery, and very useful scenery.)",
      cleric: "The rods wanted one of us each. Our brave knight took one, and I steered him off the next, for he still crackled from the first. Nobody left one empty. Even a rod can be a mercy, afterwards.",
      grovekeeper: "Rods driven in like stakes for beans. One of my lot to each stake, and whoever caught the last one rests a season. Afterwards the stakes stood as windbreaks.",
      chronomancer: "One of us to each rod, and whoever caught the last one sits this one out, still humming. An empty rod strikes everyone; a rod caught twice running strikes one person very hard. I said so beforehand. I always do.",
      alembist: "One fresh person per rod, please. Anyone still charged from the last spoils if dosed again, so keep them off it, and never leave a rod empty. The rods stay standing afterwards. Label them: reusable.",
      threadweaver: "One rod, one stitch: I sent a person to each, and kept the one still humming from the last away from the new ones. Nobody left a rod empty. The rods made good pins afterwards.",
      tidecaller: "Iron rods rose from the rock, each wanting one of us when the bolt fell. I took one, and kept out of the next while I still crackled. Afterwards the rods stood on as cover, which the sea would call a fair trade.",
      lanternkeeper: "Iron rods rose from the stone, one wanting each of us. Four rods, four of us, and I took mine. Whoever had been struck kept out of the next, still crackling, and afterwards the rods stood on as cover.",
      kenseier: "Rods stood in the stone, one for each of us when the bolt fell. I took mine, and rested through the next while the charge still clung to me. They stayed standing after, and I used them as cover. A good tool does not vanish after use.",
      houndmaster: "Rods drove into the stone, one for each of us. Took mine and sat out the next while the charge clung to me. They stand afterwards, good cover. Heel, and don't touch.",
    } },
    'Avalanche': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He stamped and boulders rolled across the summit in lanes. I stood between the lanes and let them go by, then got behind one for the walls of wind. A boulder makes a decent redoubt if you arrive first.",
      gloomsworn: "Boulders came down the summit in lanes after his stamp. I waited between the lanes, then took the lee of a stone for the wind walls that follow. Cold comfort, a rock. I have had colder.",
      thresher: "He stamped and the boulders came down in lines, like a landslide. I stood in the gap between the lines and let them roll past, humming. Once they settled I kept one at my back for the wind walls. A stone at your back is a steady partner.",
      bloodreaver: "He stomped and rocks came rolling down marked lanes. Flattened by a rock? No honour in that. Stepped out of the lane, then hid behind the boulder when the walls came. Hiding. I know. Tell nobody.",
      runeguard: "The stamp, and lanes marked across the summit. One stands between the lanes as the boulders roll, then behind the boulders for the walls of wind that follow. A stone is a stone; a stone in the right place is a ward.",
      gorgemaw: "He stamped and the boulders came rolling down the table in lanes. One does not eat rocks. I stepped between the lanes, then sat behind a boulder when the walls of wind came round. Far too dry.",
      oathbroker: "The stamp is notice served: boulders along marked lanes. We stood between the lanes. Once at rest, each boulder shelters one from the walls of wind that follow, but only once. Escrow released.",
      ironpalm: "He stamped. Stones rolled down their paths. I stood where no path ran. Then I waited behind a stone for the wind.",
      ranger: "He stamped. Boulders rolled in lanes from one side. Stood between the lanes. Then behind a boulder for the walls.",
      shadowblade: "He stamped and boulders rolled across in neat lanes. I stood between them, then slipped behind one once it stopped, and the walls of wind passed right over me. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "He stamped, and rocks came rolling across the summit like a dreadful parade! I stepped out of their lanes, then hid behind one when the walls of wind came round. A rock makes a fine parasol.",
      thermist: "Stamp. Lanes on the floor. Stand between them. Then rocks, rolling, roaring, and I ran hot until they stopped, and slid behind one for the wind. Dry rock, cool shadow. Perfect.",
      driftblade: "He stamped and boulders came tumbling down their lanes and I slipped out of their path and between them, and as they settled I ducked behind one so the walls of wind went over me.",
      marionettist: "Scene change: boulders roll on from the wings in marked lanes. The cast stands between the lanes, then hides behind the new scenery when the walls of wind come round. (Props department: flawless.)",
      cleric: "He stamped and the boulders rolled in along their lanes. My friends stood clear of the lanes, then sheltered behind the stones when the walls of wind came. I never thought I would be grateful for rocks.",
      grovekeeper: "He stamped like a rockslide starting on a hillside, and the boulders came down their lanes. My lot stood between them. Then we sheltered behind them when the wind came round.",
      chronomancer: "He stamps, the boulders roll a moment later, and the walls of wind follow on their heels. Between the lanes, then behind a boulder. I have the whole phrase by heart. Possibly from earlier.",
      alembist: "Boulders roll down marked lanes: stand between the lanes. Afterwards the boulders shelter from the walls of wind, once each, so use them before they spoil. (They don't spoil. It's an expression.)",
      threadweaver: "He stamped, and I pulled the lot of them out of the boulders' lanes. When the rocks settled, we tucked in behind them for the walls of wind. A neat seam, that.",
      tidecaller: "He stamped and boulders rolled across the summit in lanes. I waited in the gaps between them and let them go by like swell past a pier, then slipped in behind one for the walls of wind that followed.",
      lanternkeeper: "He stamped and boulders rolled across the summit in lanes. I set my lantern in the gap between two lanes and we waited there, then slipped behind a boulder for the walls of wind that followed.",
      kenseier: "He stamped and boulders rolled across the summit in lanes. I waited between the lanes and let them pass, then took the lee of a stone for the wind walls that follow. A rock is a patient ally.",
      houndmaster: "He stamped, and boulders rolled in lanes. Stood between the lanes and let them go by, then got in the lee of one for the wind walls after. Called the dogs in off the lanes.",
    } },
    'Squall Line': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The storm's edge closed on one side and opened on the other, and the dry ground walked slowly round him. The line walked with it, step for step, never outside. Marching order, in other words. Late in the walk the dry ground drew in, so I closed the ranks toward its middle.",
      gloomsworn: "The storm closed on one side and opened on the other, so the dry place travelled slowly round him. We walked with it, all of us, and none stepped out into the rain. Near the end it narrowed. We drew in toward the middle together; a tight place is a thing I know.",
      thresher: "The storm's edge shut on one side and opened on the other, so the dry patch crept round him like a shadow round a pillar. We went with it together and never stepped out into the rain. Near the end it shrank, so we huddled toward the middle.",
      bloodreaver: "The safe ground crawled round him and outside it the storm bit every heartbeat. Tempting, but no. I walked round with it and kept hitting him. Toward the end it shrank, and the storm's teeth came nearer. I stayed near the middle and kept swinging.",
      runeguard: "The safe ground slides off its centre, with a ghost of where it goes next. One walks with it round the Titan, inside its edge, and keeps striking; he is always within it. A late marginal note: the edge closes in over the last of the walk, so the reader keeps toward the centre.",
      gorgemaw: "The table itself moved round the cook, and past its edge the storm served nothing but sparks. I walked with the table, eating as I went. Inconvenient service, but I finished my plate. The table shrank toward the end, and so did the elbow room. I ate closer to the middle.",
      oathbroker: "The safe ground moves round him on schedule, and we kept to its boundary as it went. Outside it, the storm bills for every moment one lingers. I settled early. The terms tighten toward the end of the term: the safe ground narrows, so I moved nearer its middle early.",
      ironpalm: "The safe ground turned around him. I walked with it, still striking. The storm outside spoke without pause. I did not listen. Near the end the ground narrowed. I moved toward its middle before the edge asked me to.",
      ranger: "Safe ground drifted round it. Walked with it, kept my shots coming. Always inside the edge. Dry ground shrank near the end. Moved toward the middle early. No shot worth the edge.",
      shadowblade: "The safe ground drifted round the mark like a slow dance, and outside it the storm was cruel. I strolled along inside, still at his back. Not even a bruise on my manners. It narrowed near the end, and the edge came for stragglers. I slid toward the middle, still at his back.",
      pyromancer: "The safe ground wandered off round him, so I had to walk while casting, which I despise! I kept inside its edge the whole way round and kept the fire coming. At the end the safe ground shrank on me, so I took my fire toward the middle! Rude of it.",
      thermist: "The safe ring drifting round him. Walk with it, inside the edge, watch the ghost ahead. Outside, the storm was scalding! Inside, a lovely lukewarm nothing. I kept to the middle of it. Notes: the ring contracts late. Edge in, middle out. Move toward the middle before the edge reaches you!",
      driftblade: "The safe ground circled round him slowly, finally a fight that moves, and I rode round with it inside its edge, a step ahead of where it was going, never out in the storm. The ring tightened near the end, so I stopped wandering and rode the middle until it ended.",
      marionettist: "The stage itself revolved round our leading man, and the wings were full of storm. The cast walked with it, staying in the light, playing on. (The Doll never missed a step.) Toward the finale the lit stage shrank, and the cast crowded toward centre. (The Doll took the front.)",
      cleric: "The safe ground drifted round him and the storm raged at its edges. I kept my friends inside it, walking together, and called back anyone who strayed. A pilgrimage in a circle. The dry ground drew in near the end, and I gathered my friends toward its middle before the edge could reach anyone.",
      grovekeeper: "The calm patch moves round him like the shade of a tree through a summer day. My lot followed it round, inside its edge. Stray out of the shade, and the weather bites. The shade shrinks as the day ends, dear, so my lot drew toward the middle before the edge got to them.",
      chronomancer: "The safe ground circles him once, slowly, and you walk with it, inside the edge, the whole way. I could see where it would be a moment before it got there. I usually was. Toward the end the dry ground contracts, and I was in the middle a moment before it asked.",
      alembist: "A storm everywhere but a circle that travels slowly round him: walk with it, inside the edge, and keep working. Stirring while walking is a skill I have had to learn. Mostly stirred. The safe circle shrinks late. Stir nearer the middle! I stirred, and nothing spilled.",
      threadweaver: "The safe ground slid round him, and I herded my stitches along inside it, nobody straggling at the edge. A slow walk, and a tidy one. I was rather proud. It drew in toward the end, so I gathered every stitch toward the middle before it pulled tight.",
      tidecaller: "The dry ground drifted slowly round him with the storm raging at its edge, so I walked with it, my little flock inside, and called in anyone who strayed toward the rain. Near the end the dry ground ebbed inward like a tide going out, so I called everyone toward its middle early.",
      lanternkeeper: "The dry ground drifted slowly round him with the storm raging at its edge. I walked with it, my lanterns coming along behind, and called in anyone who strayed toward the rain. Near the end the dry ground drew in, so I brought the whole light toward its middle before the edge could reach anyone.",
      kenseier: "The storm closed on one side and opened on the other, and the dry ground walked slowly round him. We walked with it, all of us, never outside it. Late in the walk the dry ground draws in. I moved toward the middle before it asked me to.",
      houndmaster: "Dry ground drifted slowly round him. Walked with it, all of us, never outside the edge. Dogs at heel, keeping inside it too. Near the end the ground narrowed. Dogs and all drew toward the middle before the edge got near.",
    } },
    'Ball Lightning': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Orbs of lightning drifted off the cliff edge and across the summit. I watched each one's heading and stepped off its path. Slow is not the same as harmless. A recruit learns that with his first slow horse.",
      gloomsworn: "Lightning orbs rose along the cliff and wandered across the summit. I marked each one's bearing and kept out of its way. I know something about slow things that arrive anyway.",
      thresher: "Orbs came off the cliff and drifted over the summit, slow as drifting ash, each down its own straight line. I watched which way each was heading, stepped off its line, and went back to work. Plenty of time, dears.",
      bloodreaver: "Balls of lightning wandering across the summit. Touch one and it bursts and flings you. I stepped aside. Not every scar is worth having.",
      runeguard: "Orbs are born of falling bolts at the cliff edge, each marked with its heading. One reads every path and stands clear of all of them as they drift through. Reading, not reflex: that is the discipline.",
      gorgemaw: "Little floating morsels of lightning, drifting across the table. They burst in the mouth, and not pleasantly. I stepped aside of each one's path. Fizzy, and best left on the tray.",
      oathbroker: "The orbs travel fixed courses at a slow pace, declared in advance by the marks before them. We stood off every route. They come staggered, not together, which is the only courtesy the instrument offers.",
      ironpalm: "Orbs drifted across the summit. Slowly. I watched their paths and stepped out of them. They passed without a word.",
      ranger: "Orbs drift in from the edge on straight lines. Read the heading, step off the line. Easy shot past them.",
      shadowblade: "Crackling orbs wandered over the summit, staggered and slow. I stepped politely out of each one's way. They burst on whoever they touch, and I am not one to be touched.",
      pyromancer: "Floating balls of lightning, very nearly as bright as me! I watched where each was heading and stepped out of its way rather than argue. Eyebrows intact. Both of them.",
      thermist: "Orbs, slow, staggered. Read the heading, then step off it. They drifted past, hot and humming. I wanted to touch one. For science. I did not.",
      driftblade: "Orbs drifted in from the cliff edge, slow and dull, and I wove round each one, through the gaps between them, never into one, and wished they'd hurry up.",
      marionettist: "Glowing extras wander across the stage, slowly, on fixed paths. The cast steps out of their blocking; my Doll stepped round them with real poise.",
      cleric: "Orbs of lightning drifted across the summit from the cliff edge. I warned my friends of their paths, and each stepped aside. The young archer nearly bumped one. She is quick to learn, thank goodness.",
      grovekeeper: "Orbs drifted across like dandelion seed on a slow wind. My lot watched where each was blowing and stepped out of its way.",
      chronomancer: "The orbs come one after another, spaced like slow notes. Read each heading and step off its path. I stepped aside in good time, or will have.",
      alembist: "Orbs drift over, burst on touch, and arrive staggered: read each heading, stand off the path, don't relax after the first. I relaxed after the first. It was a lovely bang, I'm told.",
      threadweaver: "Orbs drifted slowly across, and I nudged my stitches out of their paths, one at a time. Nobody snagged on one. The archer came close. I said nothing. I did look.",
      tidecaller: "Orbs rose off the cliff edge and wandered across the summit, slow as a tide coming over the flats and just as sure. I read each one's heading and stepped off its path in good time. Slow is not harmless.",
      lanternkeeper: "On the hard nights orbs rose over the cliff edge and wandered across the summit. They are slow, and slow is easy to read. I watched each heading, set my lantern where none was bound, and stepped off the paths in good time.",
      kenseier: "Orbs of lightning rose off the cliff and wandered across the summit. I marked each one's heading and stayed off its road. It is a very slow blow, and it still counts.",
      houndmaster: "Orbs of lightning drift in off the cliff on straight lines. Read each heading and stepped off the road. Held the dogs, who'd chase a firefly into a fire.",
    } },
    'Eye of the Storm': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The wind dropped and nothing struck. I kept on him, kept the line together, watched for what came next. On the harder climb our colours turn over in this quiet; I checked mine twice, like buckles.",
      gloomsworn: "The wind fell away. The sky opened. Nothing came. I kept at him and waited for the next thing, which is most of what a watch is. On the harder climbs our colours flip in this calm; I checked mine.",
      thresher: "Wind dropped, sky cleared, nothing fell: a suspicious sort of quiet, and I rather like those. I kept working and kept looking. On the harder climb the colours swap over here, so I checked mine. Quiet is the time to see who is missing.",
      bloodreaver: "The storm stopped! No more biting from the sky. Too quiet. I kept hitting him, and on the hard climb I checked my colour twice; it flips when the calm comes.",
      runeguard: "The clouds part and the storm's steady grind ceases. One rests, mends, strikes; and on the harder ascent one reads one's charge anew, for it reverses. I footnoted it in red, and I am rarely so dramatic.",
      gorgemaw: "A palate cleanser: the storm stops, the air goes still. The cook stays at table, so I kept eating. On the harder climb, everyone's colour turns over, like a crêpe.",
      oathbroker: "A grace period: the storm ceases to charge, the sky clears, and he stays open to us. On the harder climb, every charge reverses as the period opens. Check your own before you rely on it.",
      ironpalm: "The wind stopped. The storm fell silent. He did not. I kept speaking to him in the quiet. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Wind dropped. Sky opened. Quarry still there. Kept shooting. On the hard climb, colours swap here; check yours.",
      shadowblade: "The storm stopped and the sky opened, very civil of it. The mark stayed put, so I kept working. On the harder climb our colours swap here; I glanced down to see which I'd become.",
      pyromancer: "The clouds parted, the wind died, and for once I could stand still and burn! He stays open to it. Mind, on the harder climb our colours flip here, so I checked mine.",
      thermist: "The wind stopped. Cold, still, clear. I breathed. Then, on the hard climb: the colours turn over. Check yours before walking anywhere!",
      driftblade: "The wind dropped, which I hated, everything so still, so I kept circling him anyway, cutting through the quiet, and on the hard climb checked my colour, which flips here.",
      marionettist: "Intermission, of a kind. The storm hushes, the sky clears, and our leading man stays onstage, so the cast plays on. (On the harder run, everyone's costume changes colour.)",
      cleric: "The clouds parted and the storm stopped wearing at us. I used the quiet to mend everyone fully, and said a short grace. On the harder climb the colours turn over here; I reminded my friends to look.",
      grovekeeper: "The eye came like a still afternoon in the middle of a gale. I mended my lot while the weather held off, and on the harder climb checked whose colour had turned.",
      chronomancer: "The storm stops, long enough to mend everyone: the rest before the second movement. On the harder climb the colours swap as it opens; I noticed, I'm sure, either now or earlier.",
      alembist: "The storm stops, so restock everyone while it's quiet. On the harder climb, charges reverse here, so relabel your bottles! A good moment to find the cork. Still looking.",
      threadweaver: "The wind dropped and I used the quiet to mend every frayed thread. On the harder climb everyone's colour flips here, so I re-tied the pairs. Press flat while you can.",
      tidecaller: "The wind dropped and nothing came: slack water, and the best hour to fill everyone to the brim. On the harder climb our colours turn over in this stillness, so I looked at mine, and then at theirs.",
      lanternkeeper: "The wind dropped and nothing came. I used the quiet to bring every wick back to gold and mend everyone to the brim. On the harder climb our colours turn over in this stillness, so I looked at mine, and then at theirs.",
      kenseier: "The wind fell and nothing struck. I stayed on him and watched for what came next, which is most of the work. On the harder climbs our colours turn over in this quiet; I checked mine twice. A rest is also a beat.",
      houndmaster: "Wind dropped and nothing struck. Stayed on him, watched for the next thing. On the harder climbs our colours turn over in this quiet. Checked mine twice. Easy, lads.",
    } },
    'Still Air': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Stones lit, gold and blue. I took the one matching my colour and stood at it like a sentry at a gate. Every stone needed a man, or the whole summit would pay for it.",
      gloomsworn: "The stones lit, gold and blue. I took the one that matched me, stood by it alone, and waited for the eye to shut. Not one stone was left empty. A post is a post, even without a gate.",
      thresher: "The standing stones lit gold and blue. I took the one that matched me and stood by it alone till the calm passed. Every stone had somebody and nothing was left bare, as it should be. An empty stone is a missed appointment.",
      bloodreaver: "Stones lit up gold and blue. Found one of my own colour and stood at it alone. Wrong colour, it strikes you. Empty stone, it strikes everyone, and I'd rather my friends' bruises were earned.",
      runeguard: "The stones kindle, gold and blue. Each of us goes to a stone of our own charge, one apiece, there before the Eye closes; none left empty. I stood at mine and read the other stones aloud, so no one erred.",
      gorgemaw: "The stones lit like places set at table, gold and blue, one per guest. I sat at a place of my own colour. Sit at the wrong one and it bites; leave one empty and it throws a tantrum at everyone.",
      oathbroker: "Each lit stone requires one signatory of matching charge. We assigned ourselves accordingly, one apiece. A stone left unanswered discharges across the whole summit; that is the penalty clause, and I read it twice.",
      ironpalm: "The stones lit. I went to the one that matched me and waited. An unanswered stone shouts at everyone.",
      ranger: "Stones lit, gold and blue. Took the one that matched me and stood there till the calm ended. Wrong colour, you get struck. Empty stone, everyone does.",
      shadowblade: "The stones lit up gold and blue, and each of us claimed one of our own colour, alone. A wrong colour gets struck; an empty stone punishes everyone. I took mine early and waited, unseen.",
      pyromancer: "The stones glowed gold and blue, and each of us took one in our own colour, one apiece! Standing still beside something glowing is my natural state. Leave one empty and everyone suffers.",
      thermist: "Stones lit. Gold, blue, gold, blue. Mine: my colour, alone, before the calm ends. Others to theirs. Every stone taken. Then I could run warm.",
      driftblade: "The stones lit gold and blue and I dashed to one of my own colour, nobody else's, one of us to each stone, and then I had to stand there until the calm ended, which was agony.",
      marionettist: "The marks lit up on the stage, gold and blue; each player to a mark of their own colour, one apiece, none left bare. (A rare moment of perfect blocking.)",
      cleric: "The stones lit, gold and blue. Each of my friends went to one of their own colour, one to a stone, and none was left empty. I stood at mine with a hand on the cool rock and was grateful.",
      grovekeeper: "The stones lit like lanterns at harvest, gold and blue. Each of my lot went to one of their own colour, one apiece, and every stone was tended.",
      chronomancer: "The stones light early; the calm closes much later. Each of us to a stone of our own colour, one apiece, and there before the end. I was there early. Possibly very early.",
      alembist: "Lit stones, gold and blue, each wanting exactly one person of its colour. Wrong colour spoils the dose; an empty stone spoils everyone. Labelled the stones in my head. Gold: yellow bottle. Which is silly, I know.",
      threadweaver: "The stones lit gold and blue, and I sent each stitch to a stone of its own colour, one apiece. Nobody doubled up, and none stood empty. A dropped stitch is a hole, and a hole is a draught.",
      tidecaller: "The stones lit gold and blue. I went to the one that matched me and stood there, one to a stone, none left empty, as a good tide leaves no shingle dry. A wrong colour or a bare stone, and the whole shore pays.",
      lanternkeeper: "The stones lit gold and blue. I went to the one that matched me, one to a stone, none left bare, and set a lantern on mine. A wrong colour or an empty stone and the whole party pays.",
      kenseier: "The stones lit, gold and blue. I took the one that matched me, stood at it, and waited for the eye to shut. Every stone needed someone. I let the quiet pass over me.",
      houndmaster: "Stones lit, gold and blue. Took the one that matched me and stood there till the eye shut. Wrong colour, you're struck. Empty stone, everyone is. Dogs sat down beside me.",
    } },
    'Eye Closes': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The calm ended. I was at my stone, in my colour, when it did, and the storm came back down on the line. First rule of a post: be on it when the bell goes.",
      gloomsworn: "At my stone, in my colour, when the calm ended. Then the storm returned, and I stood in it as I stand in everything.",
      thresher: "The calm ended the way a fine evening turns. By then I was by my stone, my own colour, and the weather came straight back, shaking itself like a dog come in from the rain. Rude, but expected.",
      bloodreaver: "The quiet broke. I was at a stone of my own colour when it did, as I should be. Then the sky came back, biting. Good. I missed it.",
      runeguard: "The calm draws shut, and one must be at one's stone, of one's own charge, before it closes; then brace for the storm resumed. I was early. I am always early. It is my one vanity.",
      gorgemaw: "The calm closed and the kitchen roared back to life. I was seated at my own place, the right colour. Then the next course.",
      oathbroker: "The grace period expires as the eye closes; anyone not at a stone of matching charge defaults. I was at mine. Entered in the ledger.",
      ironpalm: "The calm closed. I was at my stone. The storm spoke again.",
      ranger: "Calm shut. I was at my stone, right colour. The storm came back down on the others a heartbeat later.",
      shadowblade: "The calm ended, rather abruptly. I was already at my stone, my own colour, quietly waiting. Then back to the mark's back.",
      pyromancer: "The sky shut like a door! I was already at a stone of my own colour, posed beautifully. Then the storm resumed, and so did I.",
      thermist: "Eye closing. Check: at my stone, my colour. Then the storm back, hot and loud, and all of us moving.",
      driftblade: "The calm closed while I was at my stone, my colour, which meant landing for once, and then the storm came back and I could finally move again.",
      marionettist: "Curtain on the interlude. Each player on their mark, their own colour. Then the storm returns for the second act.",
      cleric: "The calm closed. Each of my friends stood at a stone of their own colour when it did. Then the storm returned, and so did my prayers.",
      grovekeeper: "The calm ends the way a fine mountain day ends, dear: all at once. My lot were at their stones, right colour each. Then weather again.",
      chronomancer: "The calm closes on the beat, and you are at your stone by then or you are too late. I was. Probably. Then the second half begins.",
      alembist: "The calm is ending: be at your stone, own colour, before it shuts, because the storm resumes at once. Kettle on. Unattended. Please do not mention it.",
      threadweaver: "The calm closed with every stitch at its own stone, right colour. Then I drew them in for the storm. All pinned.",
      tidecaller: "The calm turned like the tide. I was already at my stone in my colour when it did, and the storm came back down on the rest of the summit. Arriving early costs nothing.",
      lanternkeeper: "The calm closed, and the storm came back down over the rest of the summit. We were on our stones already, each in the right colour, lanterns lit. Arriving early costs nothing.",
      kenseier: "The calm ended, and I was at my stone, in my colour, when it did. The storm came back down on all of us. A rest ends, and the next beat comes. Be ready on the last quiet one.",
      houndmaster: "Calm ended and I stood at my stone in my own colour. The storm came back down on the rest a breath later. Be on your stone before the quiet runs out.",
    } },
    'Gathering Storm': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Static settled on all of us and bit, over and over. I kept close to the healers with my guard ready and let them mend the line as it went. It held by a thread. A good thread, well tied.",
      gloomsworn: "Static settled on all of us and kept biting. I stayed close to the healers so they could keep us mended. Held up by a thread, and I know the sensation.",
      thresher: "Static settled on all of us and stung, over and over, like a swarm of wasps. I kept close by the menders so they could tend us all at once, and kept working through it, humming. Prickly company, storms, but company all the same.",
      bloodreaver: "The storm gathered in all of us at once, biting again and again, more than any of us could carry alone! I kept hitting to drink it back, and the healers did the rest. Bleed first, then drink.",
      runeguard: "Static clings to every one of us, and bites again and again. One gathers near the healers, keeps every ward in hand, and expects the Thunderhead behind it. The pattern is sequence, not surprise.",
      gorgemaw: "A long tasting menu: every guest served static, over and over, and a great dish to finish. Too much for anyone to eat alone, so we sat together by the healers and they kept us topped up.",
      oathbroker: "The storm accrues interest on all of us at once, more than any single account can bear. We paid in instalments, gathered near the healers, our guards held in reserve for the final sum.",
      ironpalm: "Static gathered on everyone and spoke many times. We stood close to the healers. We breathed through it.",
      ranger: "Static on everyone, again and again. Stayed near the healers. Kept shooting.",
      shadowblade: "Static clung to all of us and stung, again and again. Even I came out of the shadows to stand near our healers. It was worth the indignity.",
      pyromancer: "Static crackled over everyone, again and again, and my hair stood straight up! I stood near the healers and let them mend me while I kept the fire coming. Rather dashing, the hair.",
      thermist: "Static on everyone. Gather in, near the healers. Then it built and built, hot and relentless, and we held together. Cold sweat. Hot ears. Perfectly consistent.",
      driftblade: "Static on all of us and biting, again and again, so I stopped wheeling for once and stayed near the healers and let them mend me through it.",
      marionettist: "The whole cast charged with static, stinging repeatedly. Blocking: everyone downstage near the healers. (The Doll endured it stoically.)",
      cleric: "Static settled on everyone and stung again and again. I gathered my friends close and mended them all as it went, never falling behind. A long, long breath of a prayer.",
      grovekeeper: "The storm gathers like weather building over the valley. I planted in all my lot the moment the static settled, so the mending grew as the stings came. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "Static, again and again, a steady pulse on everyone. I mend on every pulse and never fall behind, because the Thunderhead lands right after. I keep good time.",
      alembist: "Static on everyone, stinging repeatedly, then the Thunderhead. So: everyone close, mending poured continuously, shelf empty by the end. Worth it. Every drop.",
      threadweaver: "Static on every stitch, again and again. I tied them all together, close, so nobody took the stinging alone. Pulled it tight.",
      tidecaller: "Static settled on every one of us and stung again and again, small nicks and many. I kept close to the others, gave a little water to whoever bled, steadily, and let the rest of the tide climb behind it. Wait for the water.",
      lanternkeeper: "Static settled on all of us and stung again and again, a small nick each time. I kept the party close and the wicks full, and let the lanterns do the small work while I saved the large for later.",
      kenseier: "Static settled on all of us and bit, again and again. I stayed close to the healers and let them keep us mended. Nothing to meet with a blade, only to outlast, a bite at a time.",
      houndmaster: "Static settled on all of us, biting again and again. Stayed near the healers and let them keep us whole. Nothing to dodge, only to bear.",
    } },
    'Thunderhead': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The static broke as one great blast on all of us. I had my guard saved for that moment, the healers had the line whole first, and we took it standing. Headcount after: all present, and swearing.",
      gloomsworn: "The static broke all at once over every one of us. I had saved my strength for it and the healers had us full beforehand. Being whole is worth a great deal. I did not always know that.",
      thresher: "All that static came due at once and broke on every one of us. I knew the hour was coming, so I had my guard up and came in mended beforehand. Nothing to step out of, only to be ready, and I was.",
      bloodreaver: "The Thunderhead broke on all of us. A fine scar! I took it with everything I had left and drank back what I could.",
      runeguard: "The static's ticking ceases, which is the sign. Every ward raised, the wounded mended first, and then the Thunderhead breaks over us. As foreseen, and as survived.",
      gorgemaw: "The final course: a Thunderhead served to everyone at once. One eats it whole, with every bit of fortitude one brought to table. Heavy, but I was hungry.",
      oathbroker: "The Thunderhead is the storm's balloon payment, due from all the undersigned at once. We met it whole, every guard raised. Countersigned.",
      ironpalm: "The Thunderhead spoke. We had raised our guards and breathed first. It passed.",
      ranger: "Thunderhead broke on all of us. Guard up, mended, kept shooting.",
      shadowblade: "The Thunderhead broke on all of us at once. I had my little tricks ready to soften it, and so did everyone else. I did not flinch. I never do. Ask my knees.",
      pyromancer: "The whole sky collapsed on us in one great boom! I'd saved my best protection for exactly that moment. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "The Thunderhead. Guard up, mended, ready. Then the heat, all of it, at once! Peak reading. Off the scale. I may have laughed.",
      driftblade: "The Thunderhead broke over all of us and I'd saved my best guard for it and was mended, and then it was over and I could fly again.",
      marionettist: "The scene's finale: a Thunderhead on the whole house. The cast met it whole, every protection raised. (Standing ovation.)",
      cleric: "The Thunderhead broke over everyone. I had my friends whole before it landed, and each had raised their own guard. Not a one fell. I counted them twice.",
      grovekeeper: "The Thunderhead breaks like a summer storm finally letting go. My lot were mended and guarded before it.",
      chronomancer: "The Thunderhead lands at the end of the static, on the downbeat. Everyone whole, every guard raised. I will have undone one, at most.",
      alembist: "One great blast after the static: everyone topped up, every guard raised, brewed well in advance. If it goes wrong, stand clear. I would.",
      threadweaver: "The Thunderhead broke over the whole hem. I had every stitch mended and snug beforehand. Nothing snapped. I checked twice anyway.",
      tidecaller: "The static gathered itself and broke over all of us as one blow. By then the water stood at the crest and each of us had raised our own guard, so it fell on a full shore and not one of us went down.",
      lanternkeeper: "The static gathered and broke over all of us as one blow. Each of us had raised our own guard, and I let every lantern burst as it fell, so it landed on a party already mended.",
      kenseier: "The static broke as one great blast over every one of us. I wore the ash for it, and the healers had us full beforehand. A blow you cannot turn is best met whole.",
      houndmaster: "It broke over all of us in one blast. Healers had us full beforehand, and that was the answer. Dogs close in by the healer. Sit tight.",
    } },
    'Skyfall': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "He called the sky down on the spot where one of us stood. The nearer you are, the worse it falls. I gave ground away from it, as far as the edge allowed. A retreat on orders, my own.",
      gloomsworn: "He dropped the sky on the spot where one of us had been. The closer you are, the harder it lands. I went as far from it as the summit allowed. Even I hide from some things.",
      thresher: "The weather picked the spot where one of us had stood and brought the sky down on it. The nearer you stand, the harder it falls, so I strolled to the far edge of the summit and let it land on empty rock. An appointment nobody was keeping.",
      bloodreaver: "Thunder-belly called the sky down where one of us stood. Got as far from that spot as I could. Even I don't want that one.",
      runeguard: "The clouds open over the spot where one of us stood as he called it. The distance is the ward: everyone as far from that spot as the summit permits. A rare case where retreat is doctrine.",
      gorgemaw: "The sky itself, served on one spot, the plate where a guest had stood. The closer you sit, the larger the portion. I sat at the far end of the table.",
      oathbroker: "The sky falls on a set spot, with liability by distance: the nearer, the more owed. We stood as far away as the summit allowed, and declined to contest the bill.",
      ironpalm: "The sky fell where one of us had stood. I walked as far from it as the edge allowed. It spoke softly at that distance.",
      ranger: "Sky came down on the spot someone had stood. I was already across the summit by then. Distance is the best armour I own.",
      shadowblade: "The sky fell on the spot where one of us stood when he called it. I withdrew to the far side of the summit, as one does from an unpleasant guest.",
      pyromancer: "The sky itself came down on one spot, brighter than anything I can do! I ran as far from it as the edge allowed. Eyebrows intact.",
      thermist: "Sky, falling on one spot. The farthest point from it. Then the blast, hot even at the edge! Distance, distance, distance. Write it on my hand.",
      driftblade: "The sky came down where one of us had stood and I swept away from it, all the way to the edge, as far as the summit would let me.",
      marionettist: "The sky falls on the mark where a player stood. The cast exits to the farthest wings. (The Doll and I were, naturally, already there.)",
      cleric: "The sky came down on the spot where one of my friends had stood. I sent everyone as far from it as the summit allowed, and followed last.",
      grovekeeper: "The sky came down like hail on one field. My lot went to the farthest corner of the farm. Distance is the only shelter from that.",
      chronomancer: "He calls it on where someone stands, and it falls a few breaths later. Go as far from that spot as you can. I will have been there before.",
      alembist: "The sky is falling on one spot, and the cure is distance, as much as possible. The nearer, the worse. I don't need to test this one. Really.",
      threadweaver: "The sky fell on where one of my stitches had stood. I pulled everyone to the far edge, all together, and not one of them had to be told twice.",
      tidecaller: "He called the sky down on the spot where one of us had stood, and the nearer you are, the harder it falls. I walked out to the far edge of the summit, as far as the shore allowed, and the water followed me.",
      lanternkeeper: "On the hard climb he called the sky down on the spot where one of us had stood, and the nearer you were, the harder it fell. I carried my lantern out to the far edge of the summit and the party came after.",
      kenseier: "He dropped the sky on the spot where one of us stood. The nearer, the harder it landed. I went as far from it as the summit allowed. Some blows are for leaving.",
      houndmaster: "He dropped the sky on the spot where someone stood. Nearer you are, the harder it lands. Got as far from it as the summit allowed, dogs too. Good dogs.",
    } },
    'Heavens Fall': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The sky came down on a marked spot at his side. All of us stood in it, gold and blue both, and held it up. Each fall was heavier. On the harder climb it broke over us as it landed, and we scattered at once.",
      gloomsworn: "A circle was painted at his side. We crowded into it, mixed colours, and split each blow, heavier every time. On the harder climb the sky shattered as it landed, and each of us marched apart at once.",
      thresher: "A patch was drawn at his side. Every one of us crowded in, mixed colours, shoulder to shoulder, and shared the weight, heavier each time. On the harder climb it shattered as it landed, so we scattered like dandelion seeds. Quickly, dears.",
      bloodreaver: "The sky came down and all of us stood under it, gold and blue mixed, each fall a little heavier. Better and better! On the harder climb it broke as it landed, so we held, then scattered, wide and fast!",
      runeguard: "A column of sky is marked beside the Titan. The whole party stands in it, both charges present, sharing each blow, heavier every time. On the harder climb it breaks as it lands: hold, then part at once.",
      gorgemaw: "The last course, shared by all at one table: the sky itself. Everyone in the circle, gold and blue both, splitting the plate. Richer each time. On the harder climb, after each course the plates are cleared in a hurry: hold, then everyone to a separate table.",
      oathbroker: "The sky is a joint debt, payable by all the undersigned, and both charges must sign. We stood within it as one; each fall was heavier. On the harder climb the debt splits the instant it lands: each to a separate corner.",
      ironpalm: "The sky came down. We all stood in it together, both colours. We held it up. Breathe. On the harder climb the sky breaks after it lands. Hold, then part, wide. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Sky marked a spot by it. All of us in, both colours. Shared it. Harder climb: it breaks after landing. Hold, then scatter, far apart.",
      shadowblade: "The sky came down on a marked spot and we crowded into it, gold and blue. Sharing is beneath me, but I made an exception. On the harder climb it shattered as it landed, so I held, then slipped away from the rest.",
      pyromancer: "The sky itself! All of us stood in the spot together, gold and blue, and held it up between us! Each one heavier. Somewhat singed, but triumphant. On the harder climb the fallen sky exploded over each of us after, so hold it, then scatter, as far apart as the summit allows!",
      thermist: "Sky, marked spot. Everyone in, both colours. It lands, we hold, again and again. Each one heavier. My knees: shaking. My heart: delighted. Harder climb: after the hold, a mark on each of us. Everyone apart. Fast. My feet: ready.",
      driftblade: "The sky came down on a marked spot and we landed in it together, both colours, and I hated it a little less each time. On the harder climb it broke right after, and I drifted away from everyone at once.",
      marionettist: "The finale: the entire cast onstage together, beneath the falling sky, gold and blue. (The Doll held her corner.) On the harder climb, a curtain call: the moment it lands, each of us takes a separate corner of the stage.",
      cleric: "The sky came down on a marked spot. I gathered every friend into it, gold and blue together, and mended until my voice gave out. On the harder climb it broke over each of us as it landed, so I sent everyone apart.",
      grovekeeper: "A marked field, and the whole sky coming down on it. We stood under it, both colours, and held the roof up like a barn raising. On the harder climb it shattered as it landed, so we planted ourselves apart, like fence posts.",
      chronomancer: "The sky comes down on a marked spot, each one heavier. All of us in it, both colours, every time. I started counting. On the harder climb it breaks right after landing, so I count: hold, then everyone apart.",
      alembist: "The sky on a marked spot: everyone in, both colours, and heavier each time. The last dose is always the largest. Stir slowly. On the harder climb the dose is split the instant it lands: hold, then everyone to a separate corner, clear of the next.",
      threadweaver: "The sky came down on a marked spot. I pulled every stitch into it, gold and blue together, and held on. Heavier each time. My thimble has a dent in it now. On the harder climb the sky frayed apart as it landed. Hold the stitch, then pull every thread out, away from each other.",
      tidecaller: "The sky came down on a marked patch, each fall heavier, and the tide of us rose into it, gold and blue, to share the weight. On the harder climb it broke up like surf as it landed, so we drew out wide apart.",
      lanternkeeper: "A marked patch at his side, and the sky falling on it again and again. We stood in it together with my lantern set in the middle, and the wicks stayed full. On the harder climb it broke as it landed, so we walked our lights apart.",
      kenseier: "A circle was painted at his side, and the sky came down into it, over and over. All of us crowded in, gold beside blue, and split each fall. On the harder climb it shattered as it landed, and we parted at once.",
      houndmaster: "He painted a circle by his side and the sky dropped into it again and again, harder each time. Gold and blue went in together. On the harder climb it broke after landing. Hold, then everyone apart, dogs included.",
    } },
    'Twin Fall': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The sky split in two, a column painted each side of him. Orders: a gold and a blue to each, never two alike, the pairs apart. My partner and I took the near one and held. On the harder climb a second pair followed, a quarter turn round, and we marched to it. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The sky split, and a column fell either side of him. A gold and a blue to each, never two of a kind, the pairs well apart. We held ours. On the harder climb a second pair came a quarter turn round, and we walked to it. I have carried heavier.",
      thresher: "The sky divided, a column each side, like two halves of a dance floor! A gold and a blue to each, partners together, never two alike. We hummed and held. On the harder climb a second pair of columns followed round the corner, so we waltzed over.",
      bloodreaver: "The sky split in two and I laughed! A column each side of him, a gold and a blue to each, never two alike or it bites. Mine and I held ours, bruises shared. On the harder climb a second pair came a quarter turn on; we ran to it, grinning.",
      runeguard: "A second diagram on the floor: two columns, one either side, each wanting a gold and a blue and never a like pair. I annotated it in a hurry. On the harder ascent a second pair follows, a quarter turn round: one holds, then walks. Clearly drawn. Hard to hold.",
      gorgemaw: "Two sharing plates this time, one either side of the table. Each wants a gold and a blue, never two of a kind, and the pairs sit apart. We split the course. On the harder climb a second pair of plates follows round the table; finish one, then change seats. Second helpings.",
      oathbroker: "Two instruments this time, one drawn either side, each requiring a gold and a blue to countersign; like colours void it. We kept the pairs apart and held. Rider for the harder climb: a second pair of columns, a quarter turn round, falls due after the first. Discharge one, then move on.",
      ironpalm: "The sky split. Two columns, one each side. A gold and a blue in each, never alike, the pairs apart. My partner and I stood still and held it. On the harder climb two more came, a quarter turn round. We moved when the first was done. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Sky split in two, a column each side. Gold and blue to each, never two alike. My partner and I took the near one. On the hard climb a second pair came a quarter turn round; held one, ran to the next. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "The sky split in two and everyone was obliged to stand somewhere conspicuous. A gold and a blue in each column, shoulder to shoulder, never two alike. I managed with poor grace. On the harder climb a second pair followed a quarter turn round, and I left the first unseen.",
      pyromancer: "The sky SPLIT, one column each side, and I was in one of them, naturally! A gold and a blue to each, never two alike, or it screams. We held. On the harder climb a second pair came a quarter turn round, and I sprinted to it, hat in hand.",
      thermist: "Two columns, one either side. Hypothesis: a gold and a blue in each, never alike, the pairs apart. Confirmed! Held, warm as toast, then cold with relief. On the harder climb a second pair follows a quarter turn round: hold one, run to the next. Reading: survivable.",
      driftblade: "The sky split and for once I had to stand still, in a column, beside my opposite, one pair each side, never two alike. I held it, wheeling inside the ring as far as I dared. On the harder climb a second pair followed round, so I flew to it the instant the first fell.",
      marionettist: "Act the third: the stage divides, a column each side. Blocking: a gold and a blue to each, never two alike, the duets kept apart. (The Doll held her mark.) On the harder climb there is an encore, a second pair a quarter turn round: hold the first, exit, enter the next.",
      cleric: "The sky split in two and each pair took a column, a gold with a blue, never two of a colour, and I mended the pairs one after the other as they stood. On the harder climb a second pair followed a quarter turn round, so we walked to it together. Old knees, old patience.",
      grovekeeper: "Two fields of sky fell, one each side of him. Gold and blue in each, like rain and soil, never two alike. We stood in our field and held it. On the harder climb a second pair followed a quarter turn round, so we crossed over between falls, dear. Plant, then harvest.",
      chronomancer: "The sky split, two columns, one each side, and I had stood in one before, or will. A gold and a blue in each, never two alike. On the harder climb a second pair follows a quarter turn round; I moved a step early, as I always do. Frequently.",
      alembist: "Two vessels this time, one each side of him. Into each: one gold, one blue, never two of a kind, or it boils over. Pairs apart. We held. On the harder climb a second pair of vessels follows, a quarter turn round: pour out, then move. Labelled it. Twice.",
      threadweaver: "The sky split along a seam, a column each side. I tied a gold to a blue and sent each pair to its own column, never two alike on one thread. On the harder climb a second seam opened a quarter turn round, so I cut the pairs loose and sewed them in over there.",
      tidecaller: "The sky split like a wave on a rock, a column each side of him. A gold and a blue went to each, the pairs far apart, never two alike. We held it. On the harder climb a second pair came a quarter turn round, so we followed the tide to it.",
      lanternkeeper: "The sky split in two, a column each side, and I lit a lantern in the one I took, a gold and a blue together, never two alike. The other pair did the same across from us. On the harder climb a second pair followed a quarter turn round, and we carried our light over.",
      kenseier: "The sky split in two. One column each side. A gold and a blue held each, never two alike. I stood in mine and let the beat fall. On the harder climb a second pair of columns came a quarter turn round; we crossed after the first fell. Two beats, one breath.",
      houndmaster: "The sky split in two, a column each side. Gold and blue to each, never two alike, and the dogs kept out of it. My partner and I held ours. On the harder climb a second pair came a quarter turn round, so we went to it at a trot, dogs at heel.",
    } },
    'Grounding Rods': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Orders for the harder climb: a rod over each gold, planted wherever he stands. Put them a stride off Volkar, east and west, level with him. I watched the rods go in, then went to my partner's.",
      gloomsworn: "Hard climb: every gold one of us had an iron rod hanging above, planted wherever they happened to stand. Ours went in east and west of Volkar, level with him, and the blues came to join them. I went to my partner's as soon as it was in.",
      thresher: "Harder climb: a rod hangs over every gold one of us and goes into the floor wherever it stands. So we stood the golds a stride out either side of Volkar, level with him, east and west, and the blues wandered over to meet them. A tidy pair of pillars.",
      bloodreaver: "Iron rods over the two golds, hammered in where they stood. A stride off the mountain, one east, one west, level with him. I found my partner's rod and stayed by it. No scar. Pity.",
      runeguard: "An iron rod hangs over each gold charge, and it stays wherever they stand. Plant them a stride to either side of the Titan, east and west, in the very row he stands in; the blue partners mark where they fall and join them. Placement is the whole text.",
      gorgemaw: "The cook handed each gold guest an iron skewer, planted wherever they stood. Ours went a stride to either side of him, east and west, level with his middle, like cutlery laid properly. I joined my partner at hers. Correctly set, for once.",
      oathbroker: "Each gold signatory is issued a rod, fixed wherever he stands until the fronts have passed, so placement is everything. One a stride east of him, one a stride west, in his own row. The blues countersign at their partner's rod.",
      ironpalm: "The rods came down where the golds stood, and stayed. Two of them, east and west of the mountain, a stride out. I joined my partner and waited. The mountain waited too.",
      ranger: "Rods over the two golds. Planted a stride off the stag's flanks, east and west, dead level with it. Partner came to mine. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "An iron rod hung over me, gold that night, and stayed wherever I stood. I planted it a stride off the mark's west flank; the other gold took the east, both level with him. My partner found me there. Obedient, for once.",
      pyromancer: "A rod over my head, and wherever I stood it would stay! So I picked my spot with great care, east of Volkar and level with him, a stride out, and the other gold took the west. My blue partner joined me. Very elegant.",
      thermist: "Rods over the golds. They stay where planted. One a stride east of him, one a stride west, same row. Blue: watch them land, then go to your partner's. The heat comes later; this part runs cold.",
      driftblade: "A rod hung over my partner and she had to stand still to plant it, which I couldn't bear to watch, a stride off the giant's west side, the other one east, level with him, and I swung in through to her rod.",
      marionettist: "Props: an iron rod over each gold player, set wherever they stand. Blocking: a stride either side of our leading man, one east, one west, level with him. Blue partners join theirs. (The Doll admires good set placement.)",
      cleric: "On the harder climb the rods hung over the golds and went into the floor where they stood, so we chose our footing with care. East and west of Volkar, level with him, and the blues went to their partners. I held my breath until both were in.",
      grovekeeper: "Rods over the golds, driven in like fence posts wherever they stood. My lot set them a stride either side of Old Thunder, east and west, in the row through him. Each blue went to their partner's post.",
      chronomancer: "The rods come down over the golds, and where they stand they stay until the storm has passed. A stride east of Volkar, a stride west, level with him. The blues join a little later. I knew it mattered. It does.",
      alembist: "Where the golds stand, the rods stay. So: east and west of him, a stride out, in his own row, and no shuffling. Blues to their partner's rod. Placement is the whole recipe, and I have written it on my hand.",
      threadweaver: "Rods hung over my two golds, so I placed them like pins: a stride off either side of the old windbag, east and west, level with him. Each blue stitched to their partner's rod. Straight lines, if you please.",
      tidecaller: "A rod hung over each gold and went into the floor wherever they stood. We set ours a stride either side of Volkar, east and west, level with him, as straight as a tideline, and each blue came to their partner's rod.",
      lanternkeeper: "On the hard climb a rod hung over each gold and drove into the floor where they stood. So the golds stood a stride either side of Volkar, east and west, level with him, and each blue came to their partner's rod. Two rods, two lights.",
      kenseier: "Hard climb: a rod hangs over every gold one and sticks in the ground where they happen to be. Ours stood east and west of Volkar, level with him and a stride out. The blues came over to them, and I went to my partner as soon as his was in.",
      houndmaster: "Hard climb. A rod hangs over each gold one and sticks wherever they happen to be standing. Ours went east and west of the big lad, level with him. Blues came over to their partners.",
    } },
    'Supercell': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Three fronts: west, north, east. Each dies on a rod and leaves calm behind it. My partner and I walked our rod's far sides in turn, always within Volkar's reach, and took the cell with everyone after, mended.",
      gloomsworn: "Wind fronts came in a fixed order: from the west, then the north, then the east. Each died against a rod, with calm beyond. We circled our own rod with Volkar always in reach, and stood whole when the cell finally let go.",
      thresher: "Squall fronts come in a set order, west then north then east, each ending at a rod with still air behind it. My partner and I walked round our post, east side, south side, west side, staying on Volkar, and came in mended for the cloudburst. A slow dance, and a precise one.",
      bloodreaver: "Walls of lightning rolling over the summit: west, then north, then east. A rod stops them, and past it the ground stays dry and still. We went round our rod, east, south, west, still hitting him. Then the cell burst over everyone. A scar at last!",
      runeguard: "Three fronts, ever in one order, west, north, east, a lane of still air past each rod. Circle one's rod with one's partner, east side, then south, then west; and be whole as the last passes, for the cell then breaks over us all. I numbered the fronts in the margin.",
      gorgemaw: "Three storms in a row, from the west, the north, the east, and each rod kept a dry seat behind it. My partner and I moved round ours, east, south, west, eating at his elbow throughout. Then dessert: the whole cell, served to every guest at once. Heavy.",
      oathbroker: "Schedule of three fronts in fixed order, west, north, east; each rod exempts the ground beyond it. The undersigned went round their rod, east side, south side, west side, never leaving the counterparty. As the last front passes, the cell breaks on all of us, owed in common.",
      ironpalm: "The storm came from the west, the north, the east. The rods quieted the air behind them. I walked round ours with my partner: east, south, west. Then the storm spoke to all of us at once. We were ready.",
      ranger: "Fronts from the west, north, east, in that order. Rod stops each; still lane past it. Round the rod: east side, south, west. Never left the stag. Last front out, the cell breaks on everyone. Came in mended.",
      shadowblade: "Three fronts, and each rod left a lane of still air. West, north, east: ever that order. My partner and I circled ours at the mark's side, then took the cell on the chin, mended, like everyone else. Vulgar weather.",
      pyromancer: "Three storm fronts, every one brighter than me, rude! West, then north, then east, and a still lane past each rod. I went round mine with my partner, east, south, west, never stopped casting. Then the whole cell burst over all of us. Hair: upright.",
      thermist: "Cold: west, north, east; the still lane past each rod. East side, south side, west side. Hot: the fronts overlapping, the rush round the rod, my partner at my elbow. Then the cell broke over everyone and I ran scalding. Whole beforehand, or too far gone.",
      driftblade: "Fronts came through, west then north then east, each starting before the last had gone, and I wheeled round our rod with my partner through the still lanes, east side, south side, west side, never leaving the giant, and then the cell broke over all of us and there was nowhere to go.",
      marionettist: "A three-scene storm, always in order: west, north, east. Blocking: each pair circles its rod, east side, south side, west side, in the still lane, never leaving our leading man. Finale: the cell breaks over the whole house. (The cast, mended in advance, took it bravely.)",
      cleric: "Three fronts crossed the summit, from the west, then the north, then the east. Each pair went round their rod, east side, south side, west side, staying by Volkar. As the last one passed, the cell broke over everyone; I had them mended before it.",
      grovekeeper: "Supercell rolls in like three weathers back to back, dear: a west wind, a north wind, an east wind. My lot sheltered in the lee of their rods, east side, south side, west side, never far from Old Thunder. Then the cloudburst over all of us.",
      chronomancer: "West, north, east, each front starting before the last has passed, the same three bars every time. Round the rod: east side, south side, west side, a step early each. Then the cell breaks on everyone, on the last note; I had mended them already.",
      alembist: "Brew for a supercell: three fronts in a row, each stopped by a rod. Circle your rod, east, south, west, and stay near him. The cell breaks over everyone at the end, so the whole party gets a dose before the last front is out.",
      threadweaver: "Fronts from the west, the north, the east, one on another's heels. I kept each pair tight round their rod: east side, south side, west side, never leaving the old windbag. Then the cell broke over the whole hem, and I'd mended every stitch first. Mind the slack.",
      tidecaller: "Three fronts, one on the heels of the next: west, then north, then east. Each pair circled their rod, east side, south side, west side, never straying far from Volkar. When the last passed, the cell broke over us, and the water was high before it.",
      lanternkeeper: "Three fronts crossed the summit, one on the heels of the next: west, then north, then east. Each pair circled their rod, east side, south side, west side, close in to Volkar. I let every lantern burst as the last front passed and the cell broke.",
      kenseier: "Fronts came from the west, then the north, then the east, each dying on a rod and leaving calm behind. My partner and I circled our rod, always within his reach, and took the last blow with the rest, mended. A count of three.",
      houndmaster: "Fronts rolled in from west, north, then east, each one dying on a rod and leaving calm. My partner and I walked round our rod, never out of reach of him. Last blow, took it mended, all together.",
    } },
  },
};
